= Cosmology
{wiki}

= Baryogenesis
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

Baryogenesis is the dynamical production of the cosmic excess of baryons over antibaryons. It requires baryon-number violation, C and CP violation, and departure from thermal equilibrium, or an appropriate generalization of these Sakharov conditions.

= Cosmological perfect-fluid continuity equation
{parent=Cosmology}

For a homogeneous fluid of energy density $\rho$, pressure $P$, and scale factor $a(t)$, adiabatic work in a comoving volume gives
$$
\dot\rho+3\frac{\dot a}{a}(\rho+P)=0.
$$

= Barotropic equation of state
{parent=Cosmological perfect-fluid continuity equation}

A constant barotropic equation of state has $P=w\rho$. The cases $w=0$ and $w=1/3$ describe pressureless matter and radiation respectively.

= Constant-equation-of-state density scaling
{parent=Barotropic equation of state}

For constant $w\ne-1$, the cosmological continuity equation integrates to
$$
\rho(a)=\rho_0a^{-3(1+w)}
$$
when $a=1$ at the reference time and $\rho_0$ is the reference density.

= Flat constant-equation-of-state scale factor
{parent=Constant-equation-of-state density scaling}

For an expanding spatially flat universe with constant $w>-1$ and $a(t_0)=1$,
$$
a(t)=\left[1+\frac32(1+w)H_0(t-t_0)\right]^{2/[3(1+w)]}.
$$
It vanishes at the finite past time $t_*=t_0-2/[3(1+w)H_0]$.

= Pressureless matter
{parent=Barotropic equation of state}
{wiki}

Pressureless matter has equation-of-state parameter $w=0$, energy density $\rho\propto a^{-3}$ and negligible stress compared with its rest-mass density.

= Cosmic string network
{parent=Barotropic equation of state}
{wiki=Cosmic_string}

An isotropic network of long cosmic strings has average equation-of-state parameter $w=-1/3$ and energy density proportional to $a^{-2}$.

= Radiation--cosmic-string Friedmann solution
{parent=Cosmic string network}

Consider a spatially flat universe containing <radiation in cosmology>[radiation] and a <cosmic string network>. If their densities are both $\rho_{\rm eq}$ at scale factor $a_{\rm eq}$, then
$$
\rho_r=\rho_{\rm eq}\left(\frac{a_{\rm eq}}a\right)^4,
\qquad
\rho_s=\rho_{\rm eq}\left(\frac{a_{\rm eq}}a\right)^2.
$$
In <conformal time> $d\tau=dt/a$, the flat <Friedmann equation> becomes
$$
\left(\frac{da}{d\tau}\right)^2
=\frac{8\pi G a_{\rm eq}^2}{3c^2}\rho_{\rm eq}
(a^2+a_{\rm eq}^2).
$$
Choosing the Big Bang at $\tau=0$, its expanding solution is
$$
a(\tau)=a_{\rm eq}\sinh\!\left(
a_{\rm eq}\sqrt{\frac{8\pi G\rho_{\rm eq}}{3c^2}}\,\tau
\right).
$$

= Critical density
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Critical_density}

At Hubble parameter $H$, the critical energy density is
$$
\rho_{\rm crit}=\frac{3c^2H^2}{8\pi G}.
$$

= Cosmological density parameter
{title2=$\Omega$}
{parent=Critical density}
{wiki=Density_parameter}

The density parameter is $\Omega=\rho/\rho_{\rm crit}$. In a Friedmann universe, its departure from one measures the spatial-curvature term relative to the density or expansion term.

= Flatness problem
{c}
{parent=Cosmological density parameter}
{wiki=Flatness_problem}

In a decelerating expanding universe, $|\Omega-1|$ grows, so its small present value requires extremely fine-tuned early initial data unless an earlier mechanism drives $\Omega$ toward one.

= Inflationary solution of the flatness problem
{parent=Flatness problem}

The continuity equation gives
$$
\frac d{dt}(\rho a^2)=-Ha^2(\rho+3P).
$$
During expansion with $\rho+3P<0$, the product $\rho a^2$ grows and $|\Omega^{-1}-1|\propto(\rho a^2)^{-1}$ decreases.

= Horizon problem
{c}
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Horizon_problem}

In a decelerating hot Big Bang model, widely separated regions of the observable universe have nearly equal conditions despite having no shared past light cone. Inflation supplies an earlier era with a shrinking comoving Hubble radius, allowing a formerly causal patch to grow beyond the later horizon.

= Particle horizon
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

The particle horizon at cosmic time $t$ is the greatest comoving distance from which a light signal could have reached an observer since the initial time:
$$
\chi_{\rm ph}(t)=\int_{t_{\rm initial}}^t\frac{c\,dt'}{a(t')}.
$$
Regions separated by more than twice this distance cannot have exchanged signals by time $t$.

= Radiation in cosmology
{parent=Cosmology}

Cosmological radiation has equation of state $P_r=\rho_r/3$ and density scaling $\rho_r\propto a^{-4}$.

= Effective number of relativistic energy degrees of freedom
{title2=$g_*$}
{parent=Radiation in cosmology}

For relativistic species in thermal equilibrium at a common temperature $T$, the total energy density can be written
$$
\epsilon=\frac{4\pi^5}{15(hc)^3}g_*(k_BT)^4,
\qquad
g_*=\sum_{\rm bosons}g_i+\frac78\sum_{\rm fermions}g_i.
$$
The factor $7/8$ is the <fermion-to-boson thermal integral ratio> for energy density.

= Dark energy
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

Dark energy is a smooth component with sufficiently negative pressure to drive accelerated cosmic expansion.

= Dynamical dark energy
{parent=Dark energy}

Dynamical dark energy has an energy density or equation-of-state parameter that changes with cosmic time, unlike a cosmological constant.

= Variable dark-energy equation of state
{title2=$w(z)$}
{parent=Dynamical dark energy}

For $P_{\rm DE}=w(z)\rho_{\rm DE}$, the continuity equation gives
$$
\rho_{\rm DE}(z)=\rho_{{\rm DE},0}(1+z)^3
\exp\left(3\int_0^z\frac{w(z')}{1+z'}\,dz'\right).
$$

= Chevallier-Polarski-Linder parametrization
{c}
{title2=$w(a)=w_0+w_a(1-a)$}
{parent=Variable dark-energy equation of state}
{wiki}

The Chevallier-Polarski-Linder parametrization models dynamical dark energy by $w(a)=w_0+w_a(1-a)$, equivalently $w(z)=w_0+w_az/(1+z)$.

= Cosmological constant
{parent=Dark energy}
{title2=$\Lambda$}
{wiki}

= Cosmological constant energy
{synonym}

The cosmological constant $\Lambda$ is the constant term proportional to the metric in the <Einstein field equations>. Interpreted as vacuum energy, it has $P_\Lambda=-\rho_\Lambda$, so its energy density is constant during expansion.

= Matter density
{title2=$\rho_m$}
{parent=Cosmology}

The matter density $\rho_m$ is the <energy density> in nonrelativistic matter. In a homogeneous expanding universe, conservation of particle number and dilution of physical volume give $\rho_m\propto a^{-3}$.

= Big Crunch
{parent=Cosmology}
{c}
{wiki}

A Big Crunch is a future finite-time singularity in which a recollapsing universe reaches vanishing scale factor.

= Cosmic inflation
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Inflation_(cosmology)}

Cosmic inflation is an early period of accelerated expansion, often modeled by a scalar field whose potential energy dominates its kinetic energy.

= Number of e-folds
{title2=$N=\log(a/a_i)$}
{parent=Cosmic inflation}

The number of e-folds during an interval of expansion is the logarithmic growth of the <scale factor>:
$$
N=\log\frac{a_f}{a_i}=\int_{t_i}^{t_f}H\,dt.
$$

= Primordial perturbation
{parent=Cosmic inflation}

A primordial perturbation is an early-universe fluctuation that seeds later cosmic microwave background anisotropy and large-scale structure. Inflation generates scalar curvature perturbations and tensor perturbations from quantum fluctuations.

= Inflaton
{parent=Cosmic inflation}
{wiki}

A canonical homogeneous inflaton $\phi(t)$ has
$$
\rho=\frac12\dot\phi^2+V(\phi),
\qquad
P=\frac12\dot\phi^2-V(\phi),
$$
and obeys $\ddot\phi+3H\dot\phi+V'(\phi)=0$.

= Inflaton equation of motion
{parent=Inflaton}

For a homogeneous canonical inflaton in an expanding <FLRW metric>,
$$
\ddot\phi+3H\dot\phi+V'(\phi)=0.
$$
The expansion contributes the damping term called <Hubble friction>.

= Exact hyperbolic-sine scalar-field cosmology
{parent=Inflaton}

In a flat universe containing a homogeneous canonical <scalar field>, the ansatz
$$
\phi(t)=\phi_0\log\tanh(\lambda t)
$$
has the exact solution
$$
H=\lambda\frac{\phi_0^2}{M_{\rm Pl}^2}\coth(2\lambda t),
\qquad
a=a_0[\sinh(2\lambda t)]^{\phi_0^2/(2M_{\rm Pl}^2)}.
$$
It interpolates between decelerating power-law expansion at early times and exponential accelerated expansion at late times when $2/3<\phi_0^2/M_{\rm Pl}^2<2$.

= Inflaton action in an expanding universe
{parent=Inflaton}

For a canonical scalar field in a spatially flat expanding universe,
$$
S[\phi]=\int d^3x\,dt\,a^3
\left[
\frac12\dot\phi^2
-\frac{c^2}{2a^2}|\nabla\phi|^2
-V(\phi)
\right].
$$
The field <Euler-Lagrange equation> is
$$
\ddot\phi+3H\dot\phi-\frac{c^2}{a^2}\nabla^2\phi+V'(\phi)=0.
$$

= Inflationary scalar Fourier mode
{parent=Inflaton action in an expanding universe}

For nearly constant inflaton potential and constant $H_{\rm inf}$, the spatial <Fourier transform> obeys
$$
\ddot{\widehat\phi}_{\mathbf k}
+3H_{\rm inf}\dot{\widehat\phi}_{\mathbf k}
+\frac{c^2k^2}{a^2}\widehat\phi_{\mathbf k}=0.
$$

= Canonically rescaled de Sitter scalar mode
{c}
{parent=Inflationary scalar Fourier mode}

For $a=-1/(H_{\rm inf}\tau)$, the rescaled mode
$$
\widetilde\phi_{\mathbf k}=a\widehat\phi_{\mathbf k}
$$
satisfies
$$
\widetilde\phi_{\mathbf k}''
+\left(c^2k^2-\frac2{\tau^2}\right)
\widetilde\phi_{\mathbf k}=0.
$$

= Bunch-Davies vacuum
{c}
{parent=Canonically rescaled de Sitter scalar mode}
{wiki=Bunch–Davies_vacuum}

The Bunch-Davies vacuum selects the mode that approaches the positive-frequency ground-state mode of angular frequency $ck$ in the far past $\tau\to-\infty$.

= Cosmological horizon exit
{parent=Bunch-Davies vacuum}

A Fourier mode exits the Hubble horizon during inflation when $k=aH$. On superhorizon scales $k\ll aH$, the growing mode of a sufficiently light scalar perturbation approaches a constant.

= Superhorizon scale
{parent=Cosmological horizon exit}

A cosmological mode is on a superhorizon scale when its physical wavenumber is smaller than the Hubble expansion rate, $k/a\ll H$, equivalently $k\ll aH$.

= Scale-invariant inflationary power spectrum
{parent=Bunch-Davies vacuum}

A Fourier-space variance $P_k$ in three dimensions is scale invariant when the dimensionless power per logarithmic wavenumber interval,
$$
\Delta^2(k)=\frac{k^3P_k}{2\pi^2},
$$
is independent of $k$. For a massless inflaton mode at late times,
$$
P_k\longrightarrow\frac{\hbar H_{\rm inf}^2}{2c^3k^3},
$$
so $\Delta^2(k)$ is constant.

= Primordial scalar amplitude
{title2=$A_s$}
{parent=Scale-invariant inflationary power spectrum}

At leading slow-roll order, the dimensionless primordial curvature-perturbation amplitude at horizon exit is
$$
A_s=\frac{H^2}{8\pi^2\epsilon_VM_{\rm Pl}^2}
=\frac{V}{24\pi^2M_{\rm Pl}^4\epsilon_V}.
$$

= Scalar spectral index
{title2=$n_s$}
{parent=Primordial scalar amplitude}
{wiki}

= Spectral tilt
{synonym}

The scalar spectral index describes the departure of the primordial scalar spectrum from scale invariance. To first order in potential slow-roll parameters,
$$
n_s-1\simeq-6\epsilon_V+2\eta_V.
$$

= Slow-roll approximation
{parent=Cosmic inflation}
{wiki}

= Slow-roll
{synonym}

= Slow-roll inflation
{synonym}

= Slow-roll equations
{synonym}

For a canonical scalar field with potential $V(\phi)$, slow roll reduces the field and Friedmann equations to
$$
H^2\simeq\frac{V}{3M_{\rm Pl}^2},
\qquad
3H\dot\phi\simeq-V'.
$$

= Single-field slow-roll inflation
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

Single-field slow-roll inflation uses one canonical scalar clock whose potential evolves slowly enough for the slow-roll parameters to remain small. Its adiabatic long mode produces the <single-field inflation squeezed-limit consistency relation>.

= Starobinsky inflation
{c}
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}
{wiki}

Starobinsky inflation is an inflationary model whose Einstein-frame scalar potential can be written
$$
V(\phi)=V_0\left(1-e^{-\sqrt{2/3}\,\phi/M_{\rm Pl}}\right)^2.
$$
The exponentially flat large-field plateau supports <slow-roll inflation>, while the potential is quadratic near its minimum.

= Hubble slow-roll parameter
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

A Hubble slow-roll parameter measures the fractional change of the Hubble rate or of a preceding slow-roll parameter per e-fold. Small values make the inflationary background evolve adiabatically.

= First Hubble slow-roll parameter
{title2=$\epsilon$}
{parent=Hubble slow-roll parameter}

The first Hubble slow-roll parameter is
$$
\epsilon=-\frac{\dot H}{H^2}.
$$
For a canonical inflaton, $\epsilon=\dot\phi^2/(2M_{\rm Pl}^2H^2)\geq0$.

= Second Hubble slow-roll parameter
{title2=$\eta$}
{parent=Hubble slow-roll parameter}

Using the e-fold variable $N=\log a$, define
$$
\eta=\frac{d\log\epsilon}{dN}.
$$

= Comoving curvature perturbation
{title2=$\mathcal R$}
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

The comoving curvature perturbation is the scalar curvature perturbation on hypersurfaces orthogonal to the total matter flow. For single-field adiabatic inflation it is conserved on superhorizon scales.

= Non-adiabatic pressure perturbation
{title2=$\delta P_{\rm nad}$}
{parent=Comoving curvature perturbation}

The non-adiabatic pressure perturbation is
$$
\delta P_{\rm nad}=\delta P-\frac{\dot{\bar P}}{\dot{\bar\rho}}\delta\rho.
$$
On <superhorizon scales> it sources the evolution of the <comoving curvature perturbation> through a convention-dependent signed relation proportional to $H\delta P_{\rm nad}/(\bar\rho+\bar P)$.

= Cosmological entropy perturbation
{parent=Non-adiabatic pressure perturbation}

= Isocurvature perturbation
{synonym}

A cosmological entropy perturbation changes the relative composition of cosmic fluids while leaving the total curvature initially unchanged. For <cold dark matter> and photons, a common relative entropy variable is
$$
S_{c\gamma}=\delta_c-\frac34\delta_\gamma.
$$

= Slow-roll curvature power spectrum
{title2=$\Delta_{\mathcal R}^2$}
{parent=Comoving curvature perturbation}

At leading order in canonical single-field slow roll and evaluated at horizon exit $k=aH$,
$$
\Delta_{\mathcal R}^2(k)=\frac1{2\epsilon M_{\rm Pl}^2}
\left(\frac{H}{2\pi}\right)^2.
$$

= Scalar spectral index in Hubble slow-roll parameters
{title2=$n_s-1=-2\epsilon-\eta$}
{parent=Slow-roll curvature power spectrum}

Differentiating the curvature spectrum at horizon exit gives
$$
n_s-1=\frac{d\log\Delta_{\mathcal R}^2}{d\log k}
=-2\epsilon-\eta
$$
to first order in Hubble slow-roll parameters.

= Primordial tensor power spectrum
{title2=$\Delta_t^2$}
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

For the two graviton polarizations in canonical single-field inflation,
$$
\Delta_t^2(k)=\frac8{M_{\rm Pl}^2}
\left(\frac{H}{2\pi}\right)^2
$$
at horizon exit.

= Graviton power spectrum
{title2=$P_\gamma(k)$}
{parent=Primordial tensor power spectrum}

The graviton power spectrum is the two-point spectrum of a tensor metric perturbation. A convention may quote it per polarization or summed over both polarizations, so that choice must accompany its normalization.

= Adiabatic tensor mode
{title2=$\gamma_{ij}^{L}$}
{parent=Primordial tensor power spectrum}

An adiabatic tensor mode is a long-wavelength transverse-traceless metric perturbation whose leading local effect can be produced by a <large spatial diffeomorphism>. Its effect on short modes is therefore constrained by a Ward identity.

= Cosmological soft-graviton consistency relation
{parent=Adiabatic tensor mode}

For a single adiabatic long tensor mode, the squeezed scalar-scalar-graviton bispectrum is the anisotropic momentum response of the scalar power spectrum:
$$
\lim_{q\to0}\frac{\langle\gamma^s_{\mathbf q}\varphi_{\mathbf k}
\varphi_{-\mathbf k-\mathbf q}\rangle'}{P_\gamma(q)}
=-\frac12\epsilon_{ij}^s k_i\partial_{k_j}P_\varphi(k).
$$

= Tensor spectral index
{title2=$n_T$}
{parent=Primordial tensor power spectrum}
{wiki}

The tensor spectral index is the logarithmic slope $n_T=d\log\Delta_t^2/d\log k$. Canonical slow-roll inflation gives $n_T=-2\epsilon$ to first order.

= Tensor-to-scalar ratio
{title2=$r$}
{parent=Primordial tensor power spectrum}
{wiki}

The tensor-to-scalar ratio is $r=\Delta_t^2/\Delta_{\mathcal R}^2$. Canonical single-field slow roll gives $r=16\epsilon$.

= Single-field inflation consistency relation
{title2=$r=-8n_T$}
{parent=Tensor-to-scalar ratio}

Combining $r=16\epsilon$ with $n_T=-2\epsilon$ gives the leading-order consistency relation
$$
r=-8n_T.
$$

= Blue primordial spectrum
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

A primordial power spectrum is blue when its dimensionless power increases with wavenumber. Canonical single-field slow roll cannot produce a blue tensor spectrum because $\epsilon\geq0$ implies $n_T\leq0$, but its scalar spectrum can be blue when $\eta<-2\epsilon$.

= Quadratic-potential slow-roll solution
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

For $V(\phi)=m^2\phi^2$ in units where $3H^2\simeq V$ and for positive $\phi$,
$$
\phi(t)=\phi_i-\frac{2m}{\sqrt3}t,
$$
and
$$
a(t)=a_i\exp\left(
\frac{m\phi_i}{\sqrt3}t-\frac{m^2t^2}{3}
\right)
=a_i\exp\left(\frac{\phi_i^2-\phi(t)^2}{4}\right).
$$

= Quartic-potential slow-roll solution
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

For $V(\phi)=\lambda\phi^4/4$ with
$H^2\simeq8\pi GV/(3c^2)$ and positive $\phi$,
$$
\phi(t)=\phi_0
\exp\left[-c\sqrt{\frac{\lambda}{6\pi G}}(t-t_0)\right],
$$
and
$$
a(\phi)=a_0
\exp\left[\frac{\pi G}{c^2}(\phi_0^2-\phi^2)\right].
$$

= Potential slow-roll parameter
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

The potential slow-roll parameter is
$$
\epsilon_V=\frac{M_{\rm Pl}^2}{2}
\left(\frac{V'}V\right)^2.
$$
Under the slow-roll equations, $\dot\phi^2/(2V)\simeq\epsilon_V/3$.

= Second potential slow-roll parameter
{title2=$\eta_V$}
{parent=Potential slow-roll parameter}

The second potential slow-roll parameter is
$$
\eta_V=M_{\rm Pl}^2\frac{V''}{V}.
$$
Slow roll usually requires both $\epsilon_V\ll1$ and $|\eta_V|\ll1$.

= Effective equation of state of an oscillating scalar field
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

Suppose a homogeneous scalar field oscillates much faster than the <Hubble parameter> in a monomial potential $V(\phi)=\lambda|\phi|^n$. The <virial theorem> averaged over one oscillation gives $\langle\dot\phi^2\rangle=n\langle V\rangle$, and hence
$$
\langle w\rangle=\frac{\langle P\rangle}{\langle\rho\rangle}=\frac{n-2}{n+2},
\qquad
\rho\propto a^{-6n/(n+2)}.
$$
A quadratic minimum therefore behaves like <pressureless matter>, whereas a quartic minimum behaves like <radiation in cosmology>.

= Hilltop inflation
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}
{wiki}

Hilltop inflation begins near a local maximum of the inflaton potential. For
$$
V(\phi)=\Lambda^4-\frac12m^2\phi^2,
$$
a positive field rolls away from the maximum at $\phi=0$.

= Quadratic hilltop slow-roll prediction
{parent=Hilltop inflation}

For $V(\phi)=V_0-m^2\phi^2/2$ with $V_0$ dominant,
$$
\eta_V\simeq-\frac{m^2M_{\rm Pl}^2}{V_0},
\qquad
\epsilon_V\simeq\frac12\eta_V^2\frac{\phi^2}{M_{\rm Pl}^2}.
$$
If $\widetilde N$ e-folds remain and $n_s-1\simeq2\eta_V$, then
$$
\phi=\phi_e e^{(n_s-1)\widetilde N/2},
\qquad
r\simeq2(n_s-1)^2\left(\frac{\phi_e}{M_{\rm Pl}}\right)^2e^{(n_s-1)\widetilde N}.
$$

= Monomial inflation potential
{title2=$V(\phi)=\lambda M_{\rm Pl}^4(\phi/M_{\rm Pl})^\alpha$}
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

A monomial inflation potential is a positive power of the inflaton. Its potential slow-roll parameter is $\epsilon_V=\alpha^2M_{\rm Pl}^2/(2\phi^2)$.

= Quartic-inflation tilt-tensor relation
{title2=$n_s-1=-3r/16$}
{parent=Monomial inflation potential}

For canonical slow-roll inflation with $V(\phi)=\lambda\phi^4$, the leading <scalar spectral index> and <tensor-to-scalar ratio> obey
$$
n_s-1=-\frac3{16}r.
$$
It is a parameter-free consistency check of the quartic potential at leading slow-roll order.

= Slow-roll e-fold count
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

The number of e-folds between field values $\phi_i$ and $\phi_f$ is
$$
N\simeq\frac1{\sqrt2M_{\rm Pl}}
\int_{\phi_f}^{\phi_i}\frac{d\phi}{\sqrt{\epsilon_V(\phi)}}
$$
when the field rolls monotonically down the potential.

= Monomial slow-roll e-fold count
{parent=Slow-roll e-fold count}

For $V(\phi)=\lambda\phi^n/n$ under the stated Cambridge Tripos normalization,
$$
N_e=\frac{4\pi G}{c^4n}
(\phi_i^2-\phi_f^2).
$$
If slow roll ends by kinetic-potential equality, then
$$
\phi_f^2\simeq\frac{n^2c^4}{48\pi G},
\qquad
N_e\simeq\frac n{12}
\left[\left(\frac{\phi_i}{\phi_f}\right)^2-1\right].
$$

= Natural inflation
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}
{wiki}

Natural inflation uses a periodic potential
$$
V(\phi)=V_0[1+\cos(\phi/f)].
$$
Its potential slow-roll parameter obeys
$$
\sqrt{\epsilon_V}=\frac{M_{\rm Pl}}{\sqrt2f}
\tan\frac{\phi}{2f}
$$
on the branch where the tangent is positive.

= E-fold count for the natural-inflation cosine potential
{parent=Natural inflation}

For the cosine potential,
$$
N=\frac{2f^2}{M_{\rm Pl}^2}
\left[
\log\sin\frac{\phi_i}{2f}
-\log\sin\frac{\phi_f}{2f}
\right].
$$

= Ultra-slow-roll inflation
{parent=Cosmic inflation}

Ultra-slow-roll inflation occurs on an exceptionally flat potential. The homogeneous field velocity decays as $\dot\phi\propto a^{-3}$, so $\epsilon\propto a^{-6}$ and the second Hubble slow-roll parameter approaches $-6$.

= Growing curvature perturbation in ultra-slow-roll inflation
{parent=Ultra-slow-roll inflation}

In ultra-slow-roll inflation, $z=a\sqrt{2\epsilon}\propto a^{-2}\propto\eta^2$. The comoving curvature mode equation is
$$
\mathcal R_k''+\frac4\eta\mathcal R_k'+k^2\mathcal R_k=0,
$$
and its Bunch-Davies mode is proportional to $(1+ik\eta)e^{-ik\eta}/\eta^3$. Unlike ordinary slow roll, the superhorizon curvature perturbation grows as $a^3$.

= Classicality parameter of a cosmological perturbation
{parent=Growing curvature perturbation in ultra-slow-roll inflation}

For a perturbation $X$, a normalized commutator
$$
\mathcal C=
\frac{|\langle[X,\dot X]\rangle|}
{\sqrt{\langle XX\rangle\langle\dot X\dot X\rangle}}
$$
compares its quantum commutator with its root-mean-square phase-space fluctuations. A limit $\mathcal C\to0$ signals effective classicality.

= Curvaton
{parent=Cosmic inflation}
{wiki}

A curvaton is a light spectator scalar field whose energy density is negligible during inflation but whose fluctuations can later generate the primordial curvature perturbation.

= Curvaton mechanism
{parent=Curvaton}
{wiki}

In the curvaton mechanism, the curvaton's relative energy density grows after inflation and its initially isocurvature fluctuation becomes an adiabatic curvature fluctuation when the curvaton decays.

= Linear cosmological density perturbation
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Structure_formation}

For pressureless subhorizon matter perturbations, a Fourier mode in conformal time obeys
$$\delta''+\mathcal H\delta'-\frac32\Omega_M\mathcal H^2\delta=0,$$
where $\mathcal H=a'/a$.

= Peculiar gravitational potential
{title2=$\Phi$}
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}

The peculiar gravitational potential is the Newtonian potential after subtracting the potential of the homogeneous expanding background. In comoving coordinates it obeys a Poisson equation sourced by the <density contrast>, so its gradient accelerates matter relative to the <Hubble flow>.

= Density contrast
{title2=$\delta$}
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}
{wiki=Density_contrast}

The density contrast is the fractional perturbation $\delta=(\rho-\bar\rho)/\bar\rho$.

= Spherical top-hat window function
{title2=$W_R$}
{parent=Density contrast}

A spherical top-hat window function averages a field uniformly inside a <sphere> of radius $R$ and gives zero weight outside it. Its normalized real-space kernel is $W_R(\mathbf x)=3/(4\pi R^3)$ for $|\mathbf x|\leq R$, and its <Fourier transform> is
$$
\widetilde W_R(k)=3\frac{\sin(kR)-kR\cos(kR)}{(kR)^3}.
$$

= Linear growth factor
{title2=$D(t)$}
{parent=Density contrast}
{wiki}

The linear growth factor multiplies the growing mode of a linear cosmological density perturbation, so $\delta(\mathbf x,t)=D(t)\delta(\mathbf x,t_0)/D(t_0)$ when scale-independent growth applies.

= Linear growth equation
{parent=Linear growth factor}

For pressureless matter on subhorizon scales, the <linear growth factor> obeys
$$
\ddot D+2H\dot D-4\pi G\bar\rho_mD=0.
$$
The second term is dilution by <cosmic expansion>, while the final term drives <Jeans instability>.

= Reduced gravitational clustering strength
{parent=Linear growth equation}

Replacing the self-gravity term in the matter growth equation by $(1-f)4\pi G\bar\rho_mD$, with $0<1-f<1$, weakens clustering while leaving the background expansion unchanged.

= Matter-era growth with reduced gravitational clustering strength
{title2=$s(f)=(-1+\sqrt{25-24f})/6$}
{parent=Reduced gravitational clustering strength}

During matter domination, the growing mode under a reduced clustering strength $1-f$ is
$$
D(t)\propto t^{s(f)},
\qquad
s(f)=\frac{-1+\sqrt{25-24f}}6.
$$
It grows more slowly than the standard $t^{2/3}$ mode whenever $0<f<1$.

= Matter-dominated density-perturbation modes
{parent=Linear growth factor}

In a matter-dominated universe, the long-wavelength pressureless growth equation has modes
$$
\delta_+\propto a,
\qquad
\delta_-\propto a^{-3/2}.
$$

= Logarithmic growth of matter perturbations during radiation domination
{parent=Linear growth factor}

When matter self-gravity is negligible during radiation domination, $a\propto t^{1/2}$ and $\ddot\delta+2H\dot\delta=0$ gives $\delta=C_1+C_2\log a$.

= Freezing of matter perturbations during curvature domination
{parent=Linear growth factor}

During curvature domination $a\propto t$, so a pressureless perturbation without a self-gravity term has modes $1$ and $a^{-1}$. The surviving mode is constant.

= Linearized cosmological continuity equation
{parent=Density contrast}

For a pressureless fluid with
$$
\rho=\bar\rho+\epsilon\,\delta\rho,
\qquad
\mathbf v=\epsilon\,\delta\mathbf v,
$$
the first-order <density contrast> $\delta=\delta\rho/\bar\rho$ obeys
$$
\dot\delta=-\frac1a\nabla\cdot\delta\mathbf v.
$$

= Jeans wavenumber
{title2=$k_J$}
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}
{c}

The Jeans wavenumber separates pressure-supported modes from gravitationally unstable density modes.

= Jeans instability
{c}
{parent=Jeans wavenumber}
{wiki}

Jeans instability is the gravitational growth of density perturbations whose self-gravity overcomes pressure support. For physical sound speed $c_s$, the comoving threshold is
$$
k_J=\frac{a\sqrt{4\pi G\bar\rho}}{c_s}.
$$
Modes with $k\ll k_J$ grow gravitationally, whereas modes with $k\gg k_J$ undergo pressure-supported acoustic oscillations.

= Static-universe Jeans modes
{c}
{parent=Jeans instability}

In a static homogeneous universe, a density mode obeys
$$
\ddot\delta_k+c_s^2(k^2-k_J^2)\delta_k=0.
$$
Modes with $k>k_J$ oscillate as sound waves, while modes with $k<k_J$ have one exponentially growing and one exponentially decaying solution.

= Comoving Jeans length
{title2=$\lambda_{J,\mathrm{com}}$}
{parent=Jeans instability}

Up to a convention-dependent numerical factor, a collisional fluid has comoving Jeans length $\lambda_{J,\mathrm{com}}=(c_s/a)\sqrt{\pi/(G\bar\rho)}$.

= Baryon Jeans length across recombination
{parent=Comoving Jeans length}

Before <cosmological recombination>, <photon-baryon sound speed> controls the baryonic <comoving Jeans length>. The tightly coupled <photon-baryon fluid> has $c_s^2=c^2/[3(1+R_b)]$, where $R_b=3\bar\rho_b/(4\bar\rho_\gamma)\propto a$. Using total background density in the instantaneous dynamical Jeans estimate, the scale grows as $a$ during <radiation domination>, approaches a plateau in strongly baryon-loaded <matter domination>, and drops when <photon decoupling> removes photon pressure support. Subsequently, adiabatic monatomic-gas cooling gives $T_b\propto a^{-2}$ and a <comoving Jeans length> proportional to $a^{-1/2}$. Residual <Compton scattering> can delay that cooling law.

= Collisionless Jeans length
{parent=Comoving Jeans length}

For collisionless matter, velocity dispersion replaces sound speed in the Jeans estimate. After free decoupling, a nonrelativistic thermal velocity dispersion redshifts as $a^{-1}$.

= Cold-dark-matter kinetic Jeans scale
{parent=Collisionless Jeans length}

An instantaneous kinetic suppression estimate replaces <sound speed> by the <velocity dispersion> of <cold dark matter>. Nonrelativistic particles in thermal contact with <photons> have $\sigma\propto a^{-1/2}$; after <kinetic decoupling>, their momenta redshift and $\sigma\propto a^{-1}$. A background dynamical estimate using total density is then constant during <radiation domination> and decreases as $a^{-1/2}$ during <matter domination>. Using only the species' own matter density instead makes the self-gravitating <collisionless Jeans length> decrease as $a^{-1/2}$ throughout the decoupled nonrelativistic era. Neither instantaneous estimate is the accumulated distance traveled by <collisionless free streaming>.

= Collisionless free streaming
{parent=Collisionless Jeans length}
{wiki=Free_streaming_(particle_physics)}

Collisionless free streaming is the ballistic motion of particles out of an overdensity. It erases perturbations below the comoving distance travelled by particles while their thermal velocities are appreciable.

= Cosmological sound speed
{title2=$c_s^2$}
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}

For a barotropic fluid, the squared sound speed is $c_s^2=\partial P/\partial\rho$. A Fourier density mode obeys
$$
\ddot\delta+2H\dot\delta
+\left(\frac{c_s^2k^2}{a^2}-4\pi G\bar\rho\right)\delta=0
$$
in linear Newtonian perturbation theory.

= Suppression of matter growth by smooth accelerated expansion
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}

If a smooth component dominates the expansion but does not cluster, pressureless matter still satisfies
$$
\ddot\delta+2H\dot\delta-4\pi G\bar\rho_m\delta=0.
$$
As $\bar\rho_m$ becomes negligible, Hubble friction leaves a constant mode and a decaying mode, so structure growth freezes.

= Matter density modes during cosmological-constant domination
{parent=Suppression of matter growth by smooth accelerated expansion}

When $H$ is constant and the matter source is negligible,
$$
\ddot\delta_m+2H\dot\delta_m=0,
$$
so $\delta_m=C_1+C_2a^{-2}$. The growing matter-era mode therefore approaches a constant after cosmological-constant domination begins.

= Matter growth equation as a function of scale factor
{parent=Suppression of matter growth by smooth accelerated expansion}

In a matter-plus-cosmological-constant universe, changing the independent variable from cosmic time to $a$ gives
$$
\frac{d^2\delta_m}{da^2}
+\left(\frac{d\log H}{da}+\frac3a\right)\frac{d\delta_m}{da}
-\frac{3\Omega_{m,0}H_0^2}{2a^5H^2}\delta_m=0.
$$

= Integral linear growth factor in a matter-Lambda universe
{title2=$D_+(a)$}
{parent=Matter growth equation as a function of scale factor}

The growing solution of the matter perturbation equation in a spatially flat matter-plus-cosmological-constant universe is
$$
D_+(a)=\frac52\Omega_{m,0}H_0^2H(a)
\int_0^a\frac{da'}{a'^3H(a')^3},
$$
normalized so that $D_+(a)\sim a$ during early matter domination.

= Galaxy-formation bound on the cosmological constant
{parent=Integral linear growth factor in a matter-Lambda universe}

If a perturbation has amplitude $A\ll1$ at decoupling, it needs a growth factor of at least $A^{-1}$ before cosmological-constant domination freezes its growth. The order-of-magnitude requirement is
$$
\rho_\Lambda\lesssim A^3\rho_m(t_{\rm dec}),
$$
up to the collapse threshold and the gradual transition encoded by the exact growth factor.

= Radiation domination
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}

During radiation domination in a flat expanding universe, $a(t)\propto t^{1/2}$ and $H=1/(2t)$.

= Mészáros effect
{c}
{parent=Radiation domination}
{wiki}

The Mészáros effect is the suppression of subhorizon cold-dark-matter growth during radiation domination. The growing solution is only logarithmic until matter-radiation equality.

= Matter domination
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}

During matter domination in a spatially flat universe, pressureless matter controls the expansion, $a(t)\propto t^{2/3}$, and the growing linear density mode is proportional to $a$.

= Matter-era growing and decaying density modes
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}

In a flat matter-dominated era with $a\propto\tau^2$, one has $\mathcal H=2/\tau$ and
$$\delta=A\tau^2+B\tau^{-3}.$$
The growing mode is proportional to the scale factor.

= Cosmic-time matter density modes
{parent=Matter-era growing and decaying density modes}

In cosmic time during matter domination, $a(t)\propto t^{2/3}$ and the pressureless density contrast obeys
$$
\ddot\delta+\frac4{3t}\dot\delta-\frac2{3t^2}\delta=0.
$$
Its growing and decaying solutions are $t^{2/3}$ and $t^{-1}$.

= Matter-era linear growth factor
{parent=Matter-era growing and decaying density modes}

After neglecting the decaying mode, the linear growth between conformal times $\tau_1$ and $\tau_2$ is
$$D(\tau_2,\tau_1)=\frac{a(\tau_2)}{a(\tau_1)}
=\left(\frac{\tau_2}{\tau_1}\right)^2.$$

= Cosmological horizon crossing
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}

A mode of comoving wavenumber $k$ and physical wavelength $2\pi a/k$ crosses a conformal horizon of physical size $ac\tau$ when
$$\tau_H=\frac{2\pi}{kc}.$$

= Horizon-crossing time across matter-radiation equality
{parent=Cosmological horizon crossing}

With $a(t_0)=1$, $k_0=2\pi/(ct_0)$, and $1+z_{\rm eq}=(t_0/t_{\rm eq})^{2/3}$,
$$
\frac{t_H}{t_0}\simeq
\begin{cases}
(k_0/k)^3,&t_H>t_{\rm eq},\\
(1+z_{\rm eq})^{-1/2}(k_0/k)^2,&t_H<t_{\rm eq}.
\end{cases}
$$

= Matter-era transfer of a horizon-crossing amplitude
{parent=Cosmological horizon crossing}

A mode crossing during matter domination acquires the growth factor $(\tau_0/\tau_H)^2$. An initial amplitude proportional to $\tau_H^2k^{1/2}$ therefore becomes proportional to $k^{1/2}$ today.

= Cosmological density power spectrum
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}
{wiki=Matter_power_spectrum}

The density power spectrum records the squared Fourier-mode amplitude, up to the chosen statistical normalization. Scale dependence acquired from primordial amplitudes and subsequent growth determines its spectral shape.

= Broken matter power spectrum from horizon entry
{parent=Cosmological density power spectrum}

If primordial horizon-crossing perturbations satisfy $V\langle|\delta_k|^2\rangle=C/k^3$, remain frozen during radiation domination, and grow as $a$ during matter domination, then
$$
P(k)=
\begin{cases}
Ck/k_0^4,&k<k_{\rm eq},\\
Ck_{\rm eq}^4/(k^3k_0^4),&k>k_{\rm eq}.
\end{cases}
$$

= Neutrino free streaming
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}
{wiki}

Neutrino free streaming transports neutrinos out of small-scale overdensities. Massive neutrinos therefore contribute to the homogeneous matter density while clustering inefficiently below their free-streaming scale, slowing the growth of cold-matter perturbations.

= Cosmological recombination
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Recombination_(cosmology)}

Cosmological recombination is the epoch in which cooling allowed free electrons and ions to form neutral atoms, sharply reducing the free-electron density.

= Photon decoupling
{parent=Cosmological recombination}

Photon decoupling occurs when the Thomson-scattering rate drops below the Hubble expansion rate. Photons then free stream and form the cosmic microwave background.

= Recombination temperature
{title2=$T_{\rm rec}$}
{parent=Cosmological recombination}

The recombination temperature is the photon temperature at which the free-electron fraction falls sharply as neutral atoms form. For hydrogen it is about $0.3\,\mathrm{eV}$ because the photon-to-baryon ratio is very large.

= Saha ionization equation
{parent=Cosmological recombination}
{c}
{wiki=Saha_ionization_equation}

Chemical equilibrium for $A^++e^-\leftrightarrow A^0+\gamma$ gives
$$
\frac{n_en_{A^+}}{n_{A^0}}
=\frac{g_eg_{A^+}}{g_{A^0}}
\left(\frac{2\pi m_ek_BT}{h^2}\right)^{3/2}
e^{-I_A/(k_BT)},
$$
after neglecting the ion--atom mass difference in the translational prefactor.

= Chemical-potential balance for ionization
{parent=Saha ionization equation}

Because equilibrium reactions balance chemical potentials and photons have zero chemical potential,
$$\mu_{A^+}+\mu_e=\mu_{A^0}.$$
The rest-mass difference in the Maxwell--Boltzmann densities then supplies the Boltzmann factor $e^{-I_A/(k_BT)}$.

= Hydrogen binding energy
{title2=$E_{\mathrm{bind}}$}
{parent=Saha ionization equation}
{c}

For $p+e^-\leftrightarrow H+\gamma$, the hydrogen binding energy is the rest-energy defect
$$
E_{\mathrm{bind}}=(m_p+m_e-m_H)c^2.
$$

= Free-electron fraction during hydrogen--helium recombination
{parent=Cosmological recombination}

With singly ionized helium and helium mass fraction $Y_p$, charge neutrality gives
$$
\frac{n_e}{n_B}
=(1-Y_p)X_{H^+}+\frac{Y_p}{4}X_{He^+}.
$$

= Coupled hydrogen--helium Saha equations
{parent=Free-electron fraction during hydrogen--helium recombination}

Writing $\mathcal F=n_e/n_B$ couples the two equilibrium equations:
$$
\mathcal F\frac{X_{H^+}}{1-X_{H^+}}=K(T)e^{-I_H/(k_BT)},\qquad
\mathcal F\frac{X_{He^+}}{1-X_{He^+}}=2K(T)e^{-I_{He}/(k_BT)}.
$$

= Hydrogen-only Saha equation
{parent=Coupled hydrogen--helium Saha equations}

When $Y_p=0$, $\mathcal F=X_{H^+}$, and
$$
\frac{X_{H^+}^2}{1-X_{H^+}}
=\frac1{n_B}\left(\frac{2\pi m_ek_BT}{h^2}\right)^{3/2}
e^{-I_H/(k_BT)}.
$$

= Friedmann equation
{c}
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Friedmann_equations}

$H=\dot a/a$, a fluid with $P=w\rho$ has $\rho\propto a^{-3(1+w)}$, and $\rho_{\rm crit}=3c^2H^2/(8\pi G)$.

= Friedmann effective potential for pressureless matter
{title2=$V(a)=-\mu/a-\Lambda a^2/3$}
{c}
{parent=Friedmann equation}

For pressureless matter normalized by $\rho=3\mu/(8\pi a^3)$ in units $G=c=1$, the <Friedmann equation> is
$$
\dot a^2+V(a)=-k,
\qquad
V(a)=-\frac{\mu}{a}-\frac{\Lambda a^2}{3}.
$$
Allowed scale factors satisfy $V(a)\leq-k$, and equality marks a <turning point>.

= Critical closed matter-cosmological-constant universe
{parent=Friedmann effective potential for pressureless matter}

For positive spatial curvature and positive <cosmological constant>, the effective potential has its maximum at
$$
a_E=\left(\frac{3\mu}{2\Lambda}\right)^{1/3}.
$$
The critical case $V(a_E)=-1$ has
$$
\Lambda=\frac{4}{9\mu^2},
\qquad
a_E=\frac{3\mu}{2}=\Lambda^{-1/2},
$$
and contains the <Einstein static universe>.

= Friedmann bounce for pressureless matter and positive cosmological constant
{c}
{parent=Friedmann effective potential for pressureless matter}

When the energy line $-k$ lies below the maximum of the pressureless-matter effective potential, its large-$a$ allowed region is separated from the Big-Bang region. A contracting large universe reaches the outer turning point at positive scale factor and re-expands, producing a nonsingular bounce.

= Newtonian derivation of the flat Friedmann equation
{c}
{parent=Friedmann equation}

For a test mass on the surface of a uniform sphere of radius $R$ and mass $M=4\pi\rho R^3/3$, zero Newtonian specific energy gives
$$
\frac12\dot R^2-\frac{GM}{R}=0.
$$
With $R\propto a$, this becomes the spatially flat <Friedmann equation> $H^2=8\pi G\rho/3$.

= First integral of the Friedmann acceleration equation
{parent=Friedmann equation}
{c}

Combining the perfect-fluid continuity equation with
$$
\frac{\ddot a}{a}=-\frac{4\pi G}{3c^2}(\rho+3P)
$$
shows that
$$
\frac{8\pi G}{3c^2}\rho a^2-\dot a^2
$$
is constant in time. This constant is the spatial-curvature integration constant in the first Friedmann equation.

= Friedmann acceleration equation
{parent=Friedmann equation}
{c}

= Raychaudhuri equation
{c}
{synonym}

For energy density $\rho$, pressure $P$, and cosmological constant $\Lambda$,
$$
\frac{\ddot a}{a}
=-\frac{4\pi G}{3c^2}(\rho+3P)
+\frac{\Lambda c^2}{3}.
$$

= Newtonian fluid derivation of the Raychaudhuri equation
{c}
{parent=Friedmann acceleration equation}

For a homogeneous pressureless fluid in comoving coordinates, the <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid>[Euler equation] gives
$$
\nabla\bar\Phi=-a\ddot a\,\mathbf x,
$$
while the <Poisson equation> gives
$$
\nabla^2\bar\Phi=\frac{4\pi G}{c^2}\bar\rho a^2.
$$
Taking the <divergence> of the first relation and comparing them yields
$$
\frac{\ddot a}{a}=-\frac{4\pi G}{3c^2}\bar\rho.
$$
This local fluid derivation describes a homogeneous infinite universe without choosing a physical centre of expansion.

= Recollapse of a closed Friedmann universe with nonnegative pressure
{parent=Friedmann acceleration equation}

For positive spatial curvature, zero cosmological constant, and $\rho,P\geq0$, continuity makes $\rho a^3$ nonincreasing during expansion. The negative curvature term then prevents unbounded growth of $a$, while the acceleration equation forces $\dot a$ to reach zero in finite time.

= Strong energy condition in a Friedmann universe
{parent=Friedmann acceleration equation}

With zero cosmological constant, the condition $\rho+3P\geq0$ implies $\ddot a\leq0$ and
$$
\dot H\leq-H^2,
\qquad
\frac d{dt}(H^{-1})\geq1
$$
wherever $H\ne0$.

= Finite-time Friedmann singularity under the strong energy condition
{parent=Strong energy condition in a Friedmann universe}

If $H>0$ at one time, the inequality $d(H^{-1})/dt\geq1$ forces $H\to+\infty$ and $a\to0$ within finite time to the past. If $H<0$, it forces $H\to-\infty$ and $a\to0$ within finite time to the future.

= Radiation-to-cosmological-constant transition
{parent=Friedmann acceleration equation}

In a flat radiation-plus-$\Lambda$ universe,
$$
a(t)=\left(\frac{\Omega_{R0}}{1-\Omega_{R0}}\right)^{1/4}
\left[\sinh\!\left(2H_0\sqrt{1-\Omega_{R0}}\,t\right)\right]^{1/2}.
$$
The expansion changes from deceleration to acceleration when the radiation and cosmological-constant terms are equal, at
$$
t_\Lambda=\frac{\operatorname{arsinh}1}
{2H_0\sqrt{1-\Omega_{R0}}}.
$$

= Closed radiation--cosmological-constant turning polynomial
{parent=Friedmann equation}

If the present radiation and cosmological-constant densities are respectively $\beta$ and $1$ times the present critical density, while $a_0=1$, positive curvature gives
$$
H^2=\frac{H_0^2}{a^4}(a^4-\beta a^2+\beta).
$$
Turning points are therefore the positive roots of $x^2-\beta x+\beta$ with $x=a^2$.

= Supercritical closed radiation--cosmological-constant recollapse
{parent=Closed radiation--cosmological-constant turning polynomial}

For $\beta>4$, the turning polynomial has two positive roots and its smaller root lies below $2$. A universe expanding from $a=0$ reaches that root with $H=0$ and $\dot H<0$, then contracts to a finite-time Big Crunch.

= Critical closed radiation--cosmological-constant solution
{parent=Closed radiation--cosmological-constant turning polynomial}

For $\beta=4$ and $a(0)=0$, the expanding branch is
$$
a(t)=\sqrt{2(1-e^{-2H_0t})}.
$$
It grows as $2\sqrt{H_0t}$ near zero and approaches $\sqrt2$ exponentially at late times.

= Closed matter-dominated Friedmann solution
{parent=Friedmann equation}

A positively curved pressureless Friedmann universe has the cycloidal parametrization $a=A(1-\cos\theta)$ and $t=B(\theta-\sin\theta)$, with $B=A/\sqrt{k}$. It expands from a Big Bang, reaches maximum size at $\theta=\pi$, and recollapses at $\theta=2\pi$.

= Phantom energy
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

A fluid with $w<-1$ grows in density during expansion and can produce a finite-time <Big Rip> singularity.

= Big Rip
{c}
{parent=Phantom energy}
{wiki}

A Big Rip is a finite-future-time cosmological singularity at which the <scale factor>, <Hubble parameter>, and <phantom energy> density diverge.

= Big Bang
{c}
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

The Big Bang is the hot, dense early state from which the observable universe expanded. In classical Friedmann solutions its idealized initial boundary is often a singularity where the <scale factor> vanishes.

= Hot Big Bang model
{parent=Big Bang}

The hot Big Bang model describes an initially hot expanding universe whose standard radiation- and matter-dominated eras are governed by decelerating Friedmann expansion. Without an earlier inflationary era it exhibits the <horizon problem> and <flatness problem>.

= Scale factor
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Scale_factor_(cosmology)}

The cosmological scale factor $a(t)$ converts comoving separations into physical separations in an FLRW universe.

= Cosmic expansion
{parent=Scale factor}
{wiki=Expansion_of_the_universe}

Cosmic expansion is the increase of physical distances between comoving observers as the <scale factor> grows. Its instantaneous fractional rate is the <Hubble parameter>.

= Hubble parameter
{parent=Cosmic expansion}
{c}
{wiki}

The Hubble parameter is the fractional expansion rate
$$
H=\frac{\dot a}{a},
$$
and satisfies $\dot H+H^2=\ddot a/a$.

= Hubble constant
{title2=$H_0$}
{c}
{parent=Hubble parameter}

The Hubble constant is the present value $H_0$ of the <Hubble parameter>. It sets the local proportionality between recession velocity and distance in the <Hubble law>.

= Hubble parameter identity
{c}
{parent=Hubble parameter}

Differentiating $H=\dot a/a$ gives
$$
\dot H=\frac{\ddot a}{a}-H^2.
$$

= Comoving Hubble radius
{title2=$(aH)^{-1}$}
{parent=Hubble parameter}
{wiki}

The comoving Hubble radius is $(aH)^{-1}$. It grows during ordinary decelerating expansion and shrinks during sufficiently accelerated inflation, allowing modes to cross from sub-Hubble to super-Hubble scales.

= Hubble parameter for constant-equation-of-state components
{parent=Hubble parameter}

In a spatially flat universe containing components with constant $w_i$,
$$
H(z)=H_0\left[\sum_i\Omega_{i,0}(1+z)^{3(1+w_i)}\right]^{1/2}.
$$

= Hubble parameter for matter and dynamical dark energy
{parent=Hubble parameter}

For pressureless matter and dark energy with equation of state $w(z)$,
$$
H(z)=H_0\left[\Omega_{m,0}(1+z)^3
+\Omega_{{\rm DE},0}(1+z)^3
\exp\left(3\int_0^z\frac{w(z')}{1+z'}\,dz'\right)
\right]^{1/2}.
$$

= Hubble friction
{c}
{parent=Hubble parameter}

Hubble friction is the damping term proportional to the <Hubble parameter> in a field or fluid equation in an expanding universe. A homogeneous scalar field, for example, has the term $3H\dot\phi$ in its equation of motion.

= Hubble flow
{c}
{parent=Hubble parameter}
{wiki=Hubble's_law}

= Hubble law
{c}
{synonym}

The homogeneous expansion velocity is proportional to physical position:
$$
\mathbf u=H\mathbf r=aH\mathbf x=\dot a\,\mathbf x.
$$

= Comoving coordinate
{parent=Scale factor}
{wiki=Comoving_and_proper_distances}

A comoving coordinate remains fixed for an observer following the homogeneous cosmological expansion.

= Peculiar velocity
{parent=Comoving coordinate}
{wiki}

Peculiar velocity is motion relative to the homogeneous Hubble flow. In comoving coordinates,
$$
\mathbf u=aH\mathbf x+\mathbf v,
$$
where $\mathbf u$ is physical velocity and $\mathbf v$ is peculiar velocity.

= Peculiar-velocity equation
{parent=Peculiar velocity}

For a fluid in an expanding universe, the peculiar-velocity equation is the <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid> after decomposing physical velocity into <Hubble flow> and <peculiar velocity>. It contains the Hubble-drag term $H\mathbf v$ and force $-\nabla\Phi/a$ from the <peculiar gravitational potential>.

= Peculiar-velocity divergence
{title2=$\theta$}
{parent=Peculiar velocity}

The peculiar-velocity divergence is $\theta=\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf v$. For irrotational linear perturbations it captures the scalar part of the peculiar velocity field.

= Cosmological redshift
{parent=Scale factor}
{wiki}

Light emitted at $t_e$ and observed at $t_0$ has $1+z=a(t_0)/a(t_e)$.

= Redshift drift
{title2=$\dot z$}
{parent=Cosmological redshift}
{wiki}

Redshift drift is the change in a comoving source's cosmological redshift with observer time. It directly probes $\dot z=(1+z)H_0-H(z)$.

= Redshift-time relation
{parent=Cosmological redshift}

Differentiating $1+z=a_0/a(t)$ gives
$$
\frac{dz}{dt}=-(1+z)H(z).
$$

= Cosmic chronometer
{parent=Redshift-time relation}

Cosmic chronometers infer the Hubble parameter from differential ages of passively evolving galaxies:
$$
H(z)=-\frac1{1+z}\frac{dz}{dt}.
$$
They probe $H(z)$ locally in redshift rather than through a distance integral.

= Cosmological time dilation
{parent=Cosmological redshift}

Observed time intervals from a comoving source are stretched by the same factor $1+z$ as photon wavelengths.

= Redshift preservation of a thermal photon spectrum
{parent=Cosmological redshift}

After photon decoupling in an expanding universe, every frequency scales as $\nu\propto a^{-1}$. Therefore a Planck occupation factor retains its thermal form when its temperature is assigned the same scaling,
$$
T(t)=\frac{a(t_{\rm dec})}{a(t)}T_{\rm dec}.
$$

= Luminosity distance
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

Luminosity distance is defined by $F=L/(4\pi d_L^2)$ and includes geometric dilution, photon redshift, and arrival-rate dilation.

= Type Ia supernova cosmology
{c}
{parent=Luminosity distance}

Type Ia supernova cosmology uses standardized supernova luminosities to measure the shape of the luminosity-distance--redshift relation. In a spatially flat universe,
$$
d_L(z)=(1+z)c\int_0^z\frac{dz'}{H(z')}.
$$
An unknown common absolute luminosity is degenerate with the overall distance scale but does not erase the redshift dependence.

= Cosmological flux dimming
{parent=Luminosity distance}

Expansion reduces bolometric flux by one factor of $1+z$ from photon energy and one from arrival rate, in addition to inverse-area dilution.

= Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric
{parent=Cosmology}
{c}
{wiki=Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker_metric}

= FLRW metric
{c}
{synonym}

The FLRW metric is the homogeneous and isotropic spacetime metric with scale factor $a(t)$ and constant spatial curvature $k$.

= Cosmic time
{parent=Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric}

Cosmic time is the <proper time> measured by observers comoving with the homogeneous cosmological fluid. Constant-cosmic-time hypersurfaces are the homogeneous spatial slices of an FLRW spacetime.

= Milne universe
{c}
{parent=Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric}
{wiki=Milne_model}

The Milne universe is the empty $k=-1$, $\Lambda=0$ FLRW model with scale factor proportional to proper time. It is a hyperbolic-coordinate description of the interior of a future light cone in <Minkowski spacetime>.

= Spatially flat FLRW metric
{parent=Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric}
{c}

In Cartesian comoving coordinates and signature $(-,+,+,+)$, a spatially flat FLRW universe has
$$
ds^2=-c^2dt^2+a^2(t)(dx^2+dy^2+dz^2).
$$

= Spatial curvature of an FLRW universe
{parent=Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric}

The cases $k>0$, $k=0$, and $k<0$ give spherical, Euclidean, and hyperbolic constant-curvature spatial slices.

= Proper distance in cosmology
{parent=Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric}

Proper distance is the spatial metric length measured along a fixed-cosmic-time slice.

= Conformal time
{parent=Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric}
{wiki}

Conformal time satisfies $d\tau=dt/a(t)$ and factors the FLRW metric into $a^2(\tau)$ times a static conformal metric.

= Conformal time during de Sitter expansion
{c}
{parent=Conformal time}

For the <de Sitter scale factor in flat slicing>
$$
a(t)=a_0e^{H_{\rm inf}t},
$$
choose the additive constant so that
$$
\tau=-\frac1{a_0H_{\rm inf}}e^{-H_{\rm inf}t}
=-\frac1{aH_{\rm inf}}.
$$
An eternal expanding patch has $-\infty<\tau<0$.

= Comoving radial distance
{parent=Conformal time}
{wiki=Comoving_and_proper_distances}

Comoving radial distance uses the coordinate $\chi=\int dr/\sqrt{1-kr^2}$ and removes the radial curvature factor.

= Radial null geodesic in FLRW spacetime
{parent=Conformal time}

In conformal coordinates a radial light ray obeys $d\chi/d\tau=\pm c$, so the cosmological light cone is at forty-five degrees after setting $c=1$.

= Cosmological continuity equation
{parent=Cosmology}

Energy conservation in FLRW gives $\dot\rho=-3H(\rho+P)$ when $\rho$ and $P$ are energy density and pressure.

= Equation-of-state parameter
{parent=Cosmological continuity equation}

A cosmological fluid with constant equation-of-state parameter $w$ has $P=w\rho$ and $\rho\propto a^{-3(1+w)}$.

= Quintessence
{parent=Equation-of-state parameter}
{wiki=Quintessence_(physics)}

Quintessence is dynamical dark energy with negative pressure, conventionally $-1<w<-1/3$.

= Quintessence scale-factor solution
{parent=Quintessence}

For $w=-2/3$, zero cosmological constant, and negative curvature, $a(t)=\sqrt\beta\,t+\gamma t^2/4$ with density and curvature parameters $\gamma,\beta>0$.

= Quintessence-dominated universe
{parent=Quintessence}

For $w=-2/3$, a quintessence-dominated scale factor grows quadratically and $tH$ approaches two.

= Age of an FLRW universe
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Age_of_the_universe}

The age is the proper cosmic time elapsed from the scale-factor singularity $a=0$ to the present epoch.

= Hubble time
{parent=Age of an FLRW universe}
{c}
{wiki}

The Hubble time $H_0^{-1}$ is the expansion timescale inferred from the present Hubble parameter.

= Curvature-dominated universe
{parent=Cosmology}

In a curvature-dominated open FLRW regime, the scale factor is approximately linear in cosmic time and $tH$ approaches one.

= Deceleration parameter
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

The deceleration parameter is $q=-a\ddot a/\dot a^2$; negative values describe accelerated expansion.

= Accelerating universe
{parent=Deceleration parameter}
{wiki=Accelerating_expansion_of_the_universe}

= Accelerating expansion
{synonym}

An accelerating universe has $\ddot a>0$, equivalently $q<0$ while expansion continues.

= Accelerated contraction
{parent=Deceleration parameter}

Accelerated contraction has $H<0$ and $\ddot a<0$, so the magnitude $|\dot a|$ increases. Since $\ddot a/a=H^2(1-\epsilon)$, it corresponds to a first Hubble slow-roll parameter $\epsilon>1$.

= Big Bang nucleosynthesis
{parent=Cosmology}
{c}
{wiki}

Big Bang nucleosynthesis formed the light nuclei during the first minutes of cosmic expansion after weak freeze-out and the deuterium bottleneck.

= Neutron-proton chemical equilibrium
{parent=Big Bang nucleosynthesis}

With negligible lepton chemical potentials, weak equilibrium gives $n_n/n_p\simeq e^{-Q/(k_BT)}$, where $Q=(m_n-m_p)c^2$.

= Cosmological weak freeze-out
{parent=Big Bang nucleosynthesis}

Weak neutron--proton conversion freezes out when its interaction rate falls below the Hubble expansion rate.

= Freeze-out temperature and interaction strength
{parent=Cosmological weak freeze-out}

If an interaction-to-expansion ratio scales as $(T/\kappa)^3$, increasing $\kappa$ raises the temperature at which the ratio drops through one.

= Deuterium bottleneck
{parent=Big Bang nucleosynthesis}
{wiki}

Light nuclei cannot accumulate until cooling makes deuterium sufficiently resistant to photodissociation, delaying nucleosynthesis after weak freeze-out.

= Deuterium equilibrium abundance
{parent=Deuterium bottleneck}

For chemical equilibrium $D\leftrightarrow n+p$, Maxwell--Boltzmann densities and $\mu_D=\mu_n+\mu_p$ give, after taking $m_n\simeq m_p$ and $m_D\simeq2m_p$,
$$
\frac{n_D}{n_pn_n}
\simeq
\left(\frac{\pi m_pk_BT}{h^2}\right)^{-3/2}
 e^{B_D/(k_BT)}.
$$
Writing $X_i=n_i/n_B$, $n_B=\eta n_\gamma$, and $n_\gamma=16\pi\zeta(3)(k_BT)^3/(hc)^3$ gives
$$
\frac{X_D}{X_pX_n}
\simeq\frac{16\zeta(3)}{\sqrt\pi}\eta
\left(\frac{k_BT}{m_pc^2}\right)^{3/2}
 e^{B_D/(k_BT)}.
$$
The very small baryon-to-photon ratio suppresses deuterium until $T$ is far below the binding-energy scale.

= Baryon-density effect on primordial helium
{parent=Deuterium equilibrium abundance}

Increasing the baryon-to-photon ratio raises the equilibrium deuterium abundance at fixed temperature, so the deuterium bottleneck ends earlier. Fewer neutrons decay before nuclear burning, and neutron-limited helium synthesis produces a larger primordial helium mass fraction if weak freeze-out is unchanged.

= Free-neutron decay after freeze-out
{parent=Deuterium bottleneck}

Between weak freeze-out and deuterium formation, beta decay lowers the surviving free-neutron fraction.

= Primordial helium mass fraction
{parent=Big Bang nucleosynthesis}

If almost all neutrons enter helium-4 and $r=n_n/n_p$, its primordial mass fraction is $Y_p\simeq2r/(1+r)$.

= Neutron-limited helium synthesis
{parent=Primordial helium mass fraction}

When protons outnumber neutrons, helium-4 production is neutron-limited and produces about one helium nucleus per two neutrons.

= Primordial composition and stellar lifetime
{parent=Primordial helium mass fraction}

Increasing primordial helium at the expense of hydrogen reduces fuel for long-lived main-sequence hydrogen burning and changes subsequent stellar evolution.

= Baryon-to-photon ratio
{title2=$\eta$}
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

The baryon-to-photon ratio is the number density of baryons divided by the number density of photons. In the present universe it is of order $10^{-9}$.

= Cosmological entropy conservation
{parent=Cosmology}

For an adiabatically expanding homogeneous universe, the <entropy density> satisfies
$$
\nabla_\mu(su^\mu)=0,
\qquad
\dot s+3Hs=0,
$$
so the entropy $sa^3$ in a comoving volume is constant.

= Effective number of relativistic degrees of freedom
{title2=$g_{*s}$}
{parent=Cosmological entropy conservation}

The effective number of relativistic entropy degrees of freedom is defined by
$$
s=\frac{2\pi^2}{45}g_{*s}T^3.
$$
A relativistic bosonic degree of freedom contributes one, while a relativistic fermionic degree of freedom contributes $7/8$ when all species share a temperature.

= Thermal decoupling in cosmology
{parent=Cosmological entropy conservation}

A particle species thermally decouples when its interaction rate falls below the <Hubble parameter>. Its collisionless relativistic distribution subsequently redshifts with temperature proportional to $a^{-1}$, while annihilations among still-coupled species can heat the remaining plasma.

= Kinetic decoupling
{parent=Thermal decoupling in cosmology}

Kinetic decoupling occurs when momentum-exchange collisions cease to maintain the <temperature> and <velocity dispersion> of a particle species in equilibrium with a surrounding bath. Afterwards, a freely moving nonrelativistic particle has momentum proportional to $a^{-1}$ and effective kinetic <temperature> proportional to $a^{-2}$, where $a$ is the <scale factor>.

= Temperature of a decoupled relativistic relic
{parent=Thermal decoupling in cosmology}

If a relativistic species decouples when the coupled plasma has $g_{*s,\mathrm{dec}}$ entropy degrees of freedom, then after the plasma reaches $g_{*s}$ degrees of freedom,
$$
\frac{T_{\mathrm{relic}}}{T}
=\left(\frac{g_{*s}}{g_{*s,\mathrm{dec}}}\right)^{1/3}.
$$
The ratio follows from separate redshifting of the decoupled species and <cosmological entropy conservation> in the coupled plasma.

= Cosmic neutrino background
{c}
{parent=Thermal decoupling in cosmology}
{wiki}

The cosmic neutrino background is the relic population of neutrinos that thermally decoupled before electron-positron annihilation. Entropy transfer to photons gives
$$
\frac{T_\nu}{T_\gamma}=\left(\frac4{11}\right)^{1/3}.
$$

= Relic-neutrino energy density
{parent=Cosmic neutrino background}

For one relic-neutrino species with two internal states, physical momentum $p$, comoving temperature $T_\nu$, and mass $m_\nu$,
$$
\rho_\nu=\frac1{\pi^2}\int_0^\infty
\frac{p^2\sqrt{p^2+m_\nu^2}}{e^{ap/T_\nu}+1}\,dp.
$$
It approaches $7\pi^2(T_\nu/a)^4/120$ while relativistic and $n_\nu m_\nu$ after becoming nonrelativistic.

= Late-time relic-neutrino speed
{parent=Relic-neutrino energy density}

Expanding the <relic-neutrino energy density> at $T_\nu/a\ll m_\nu$ gives
$$
\langle v^2\rangle^{1/2}\simeq
\sqrt{\frac{15\zeta(5)}{\zeta(3)}}\frac{T_\nu}{am_\nu}.
$$
The thermal speed decreases as $a^{-1}$ after the neutrinos become nonrelativistic.

= Cosmic neutrino background last-scattering surface
{parent=Cosmic neutrino background}

The cosmic neutrino background last-scattering surface is the locus from which relic neutrinos that reach an observer underwent their final appreciable scattering. Although <neutrino decoupling> occurs before <photon decoupling>, a massive neutrino travels slower than light after becoming nonrelativistic, so its last-scattering surface need not be farther away than the <cosmic microwave background> last-scattering surface.

= Neutrino decoupling
{parent=Cosmic neutrino background}
{wiki}

Neutrino decoupling occurs when weak interactions become slower than cosmic expansion. The neutrino distribution then free streams and cools as $T_\nu\propto a^{-1}$.

= Electron-positron annihilation in cosmology
{parent=Neutrino decoupling}

After neutrino decoupling, electron-positron annihilation transfers entropy to photons but not to neutrinos. Comparing the electromagnetic entropy degrees of freedom before and after annihilation gives $T_\nu/T_\gamma=(4/11)^{1/3}$.

= Electron-positron equilibrium pair abundance below the electron mass
{parent=Electron-positron annihilation in cosmology}

For $T\ll m_e$ and negligible chemical potentials, the combined equilibrium density of electrons and positrons divided by the <photon number density> is
$$
\frac{n_{e^-}+n_{e^+}}{n_\gamma}
=\frac{2\pi^2}{\zeta(3)}
\left(\frac{m_e}{2\pi T}\right)^{3/2}e^{-m_e/T}.
$$
The exponential suppression eventually makes pair equilibrium incompatible with the residual electrons required by <charge neutrality>.

= Neutrino degeneracy parameter
{title2=$\xi_\nu$}
{parent=Cosmic neutrino background}

The neutrino degeneracy parameter is the dimensionless chemical potential
$$
\xi_\nu=\frac{\mu_\nu}{T_\nu}.
$$
In thermal equilibrium the corresponding antineutrino has chemical potential $-\mu_\nu$.

= Degenerate relic neutrino
{parent=Neutrino degeneracy parameter}

A degenerate relic neutrino has $\xi_\nu\gg1$. In the ultrarelativistic limit its <Fermi-Dirac distribution> approaches a filled momentum sphere, giving for one internal state
$$
\rho_\nu\simeq\frac{\mu_\nu^4}{8\pi^2},
$$
while the antineutrino density is exponentially suppressed.

= Cosmological lepton asymmetry
{parent=Neutrino degeneracy parameter}

A nonzero neutrino chemical potential produces unequal neutrino and antineutrino densities and hence a cosmological lepton asymmetry. It changes the radiation density, and an electron-neutrino asymmetry also changes neutron--proton weak equilibrium during <Big Bang nucleosynthesis>.

= Effective number of neutrino species
{title2=$N_{\rm eff}$}
{parent=Cosmic neutrino background}
{wiki}

The effective number of neutrino species parametrizes radiation beyond photons through
$$
\rho_r=\frac{\pi^2}{30}\left[2+\frac78\,2
\left(\frac4{11}\right)^{4/3}N_{\rm eff}\right]T_\gamma^4.
$$

= Contribution of a decoupled real scalar to Neff
{title2=$\Delta N_{\rm eff}$}
{parent=Effective number of neutrino species}

If a real relativistic scalar decouples when the other coupled particles have entropy degrees of freedom $g_{*s}(T_d)$, then
$$
\Delta N_{\rm eff}=\frac47
\left(\frac{43}{4g_{*s}(T_d)}\right)^{4/3}.
$$
Earlier decoupling and the subsequent annihilation of more coupled species make the scalar colder and suppress its contribution.

= Linear cosmological perturbation theory
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Cosmological_perturbation_theory}

Linear cosmological perturbation theory expands the metric and matter fields to first order about a homogeneous and isotropic <Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric>.

= Adiabatic mode
{parent=Linear cosmological perturbation theory}

An adiabatic mode is a long-wavelength physical perturbation whose leading local field configuration is generated by a residual gauge transformation. Its action on shorter modes produces a cosmological soft theorem or consistency relation.

= Tensor cosmological perturbation
{title2=$h_{ij}$}
{parent=Linear cosmological perturbation theory}

A tensor cosmological perturbation is the transverse, trace-free part of a metric perturbation about a <Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric>. In conformal coordinates, its spatial metric has the form $g_{ij}=a^2(\delta_{ij}+h_{ij})$, with $\partial_i h_{ij}=0$ and $h_{ii}=0$. Its two independent polarizations describe a <gravitational wave>. <Neutrino tensor anisotropic stress> can damp its evolution through <neutrino free streaming>.

= Neutrino tensor anisotropic stress
{title2=$\pi_\nu^{ij}$}
{parent=Tensor cosmological perturbation}

For massless <neutrinos> with a temperature perturbation $\Theta$ and background <energy density> $\bar\rho_\nu$, the physical trace-free spatial <stress-energy tensor> is
$$
\pi_\nu^{ij}=4\bar\rho_\nu\int\frac{d\Omega_e}{4\pi}
\Theta\left(e^ie^j-\frac13\delta^{ij}\right).
$$
Some conventions define $\Pi^{ij}=-\pi_\nu^{ij}$ and correspondingly reverse the sign of the stress source in the gravitational-wave equation.

= Neutrino tensor free-streaming kernel
{title2=$K(x)=j_2(x)/x^2$}
{parent=Neutrino tensor anisotropic stress}

Projecting free-streaming <neutrinos> onto the tensor <spherical harmonic> gives
$$
K(x)=\frac1{16}\int_{-1}^{1}(1-\mu^2)^2e^{-ix\mu}\,d\mu
=\frac{j_2(x)}{x^2},\qquad K(0)=\frac1{15}.
$$
Here $j_2$ is a <spherical Bessel function>. The continuous value at zero follows by integrating $(1-\mu^2)^2$, whose integral is $16/15$.

= Gravitational-wave damping by free-streaming neutrinos
{parent=Neutrino tensor anisotropic stress}

For either tensor <helicity>, <neutrino free streaming> produces the memory equation
$$
\ddot h+2\mathcal H\dot h+k^2h
=-24\mathcal H^2f_\nu\int_0^\eta
K(k(\eta-\eta'))\dot h(\eta')\,d\eta',
\qquad
f_\nu=\frac{\bar\rho_\nu}{\bar\rho_{\rm tot}}.
$$
The <neutrino tensor free-streaming kernel> encodes the angular propagation of the perturbation. Energy transferred to neutrino directional anisotropy damps the oscillation amplitude of a <gravitational wave> after horizon entry. This effect was calculated by https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0306304[Weinberg].

= Scalar cosmological perturbation
{parent=Linear cosmological perturbation theory}

A scalar cosmological perturbation transforms as a scalar under spatial rotations and can be represented using scalar metric potentials, density and pressure perturbations, and the longitudinal part of the fluid velocity.

= Synchronous gauge in cosmology
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}
{wiki=Synchronous_gauge}

Synchronous gauge sets the lapse and shift perturbations to zero, $h_{0\mu}=0$. Its coordinates follow freely falling observers, but residual coordinate freedom produces gauge modes that must be distinguished from physical perturbations.

= Cosmological Euler equation in synchronous gauge
{c}
{parent=Synchronous gauge in cosmology}

For scalar perturbations of a generic fluid in cosmic time, momentum conservation in <synchronous gauge in cosmology> gives
$$
\delta P+\nabla^2\pi^S
+\partial_t[(\bar\rho+\bar P)\delta u]
+3H(\bar\rho+\bar P)\delta u=0.
$$

= Newtonian gauge in cosmology
{c}
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}
{wiki=Newtonian_gauge}

Newtonian gauge removes scalar shear and writes scalar metric perturbations in terms of gravitational potentials. The fluid Euler equation then contains an explicit gradient of the Newtonian potential.

= Cosmological Euler equation
{parent=Newtonian gauge in cosmology}

For pressureless matter in conformal time and Newtonian gauge,
$$
\mathbf v'+\mathcal H\mathbf v+(\mathbf v\mathbin\cdot\nabla)\mathbf v
=-\nabla\phi.
$$
Its linear curl implies that vorticity decays as $a^{-1}$.

= Cosmological Poisson equation
{parent=Newtonian gauge in cosmology}

On subhorizon scales, the Newtonian potential sourced by the matter density contrast obeys
$$
\nabla^2\phi=\frac32\mathcal H^2\Omega_m\delta.
$$

= Scalar velocity potential
{title2=$\delta u$}
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}

The longitudinal velocity perturbation of a cosmological fluid can be represented by a scalar velocity potential through $\delta T^0{}_i=(\bar\rho+\bar P)\partial_i\delta u$.

= Scalar anisotropic stress
{title2=$\pi^S$}
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}

Scalar anisotropic stress is the trace-free scalar part of the spatial stress perturbation. Under a common convention it enters as $\delta T^i{}_j=\delta P\,\delta^i_j+\partial^i\partial_j\pi^S$.

= Flat gauge in cosmology
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}

Flat gauge sets the scalar perturbation of the spatial metric to zero. Scalar fluctuations then reside in the lapse, shift, and matter fields, making the field fluctuation directly visible while the lapse and shift remain constrained variables.

= Cosmological transfer function
{parent=Linear cosmological perturbation theory}

A cosmological transfer function relates a late perturbation to a specified primordial perturbation mode by mode. It records the scale-dependent evolution caused by horizon entry, pressure, free streaming, and changes in the dominant cosmic component.

= Radiation acoustic transfer function
{parent=Cosmological transfer function}

During matter domination with nearly constant gravitational potential, a radiation density perturbation obeys a forced acoustic equation. The observable <Sachs-Wolfe combination> is the homogeneous oscillatory part.

= Cold-dark-matter transfer function
{title2=CDM transfer function}
{parent=Cosmological transfer function}

At fixed time after equality, the comoving cold-dark-matter density contrast per unit primordial curvature grows as $k^2$ for $k\ll k_{\rm eq}$ and only logarithmically for $k\gg k_{\rm eq}$. Small-scale modes entered during radiation domination and could grow only logarithmically until equality.

= Baryon transfer function
{parent=Cosmological transfer function}

The baryon transfer function retains acoustic oscillations generated while baryons belonged to the <photon-baryon fluid>. After recombination, baryons fall into cold-dark-matter potential wells, reducing the relative perturbation while leaving a small oscillatory imprint.

= Baryon--cold-dark-matter relative density mode
{title2=$D=\delta_c-\delta_b$}
{parent=Baryon transfer function}

After recombination and when pressure is negligible, cold dark matter and baryons feel the same gravitational acceleration. Their density-contrast difference therefore obeys
$$
\ddot D+2H\dot D=0.
$$
During matter domination $D=D_0+D_1a^{-1/2}$, whereas the growing total-matter mode is proportional to $a$, so $\delta_c/\delta_b\to1$.

= Field theory in cosmology
{parent=Cosmology}

Field theory in cosmology applies classical and quantum fields to the homogeneous universe and its perturbations.

= P(X, phi) scalar field theory
{title2=$P(X,\phi)$ theory}
{c}
{parent=Field theory in cosmology}
{wiki=K-essence}

A $P(X,\phi)$ scalar theory has Lagrangian density $P$ depending on the field and $X=-g^{\mu\nu}\partial_\mu\phi\partial_\nu\phi/2$. It includes canonical scalar theory and models with noncanonical kinetic interactions.

= Sound speed of a P(X, phi) scalar perturbation
{title2=$c_s^2$}
{c}
{parent=P(X, phi) scalar field theory}

On a homogeneous background with $X=\bar X$, a $P(X,\phi)$ perturbation has sound speed
$$
c_s^2=\frac{P_X}{P_X+2XP_{XX}}.
$$
The denominator is its time-kinetic coefficient and $P_X$ is its spatial-gradient coefficient.

= Cubic curvature action for a P(X, phi) scalar field
{parent=P(X, phi) scalar field theory}

In the limit where <scalar field> fluctuations dominate metric fluctuations, put $\zeta=-H\delta\phi/\dot{\bar\phi}$ and neglect derivatives of slowly varying background coefficients. Splitting $\delta X=\delta X_1+\delta X_2$ gives
$$
\delta X_1=-\frac{2\bar X}{H}\dot\zeta,\qquad
\delta X_2=\frac{\bar X}{H^2}\left(\dot\zeta^2-a^{-2}(\partial_i\zeta)^2\right).
$$
The cubic terms $P_{XX}\delta X_1\delta X_2+P_{XXX}\delta X_1^3/6$ therefore yield
$$
S_3=\int dt\,d^3x\,\frac{a^3\epsilon(1-c_s^2)}{Hc_s^2}
\left[a^{-2}\dot\zeta(\partial_i\zeta)^2+\mathcal A\dot\zeta^3\right],
\qquad
\mathcal A=-1-\frac{2\bar X P_{XXX}}{3P_{XX}}.
$$
The expression before factoring remains well-defined when $P_{XX}=0$. These interactions can generate substantial <primordial non-Gaussianity> when the <sound speed of a P(X, phi) scalar perturbation> is small.

= ADM momentum constraint for a P(X, phi) scalar field
{title2=ADM momentum constraint for a $P(X,\phi)$ scalar field}
{c}
{parent=P(X, phi) scalar field theory}

Varying the shift in the <3+1 decomposition of spacetime> gives a nondynamical momentum constraint. For a $P(X,\phi)$ field it equates the spatial divergence of $K_i{}^j-\delta_i^jK$ to the scalar momentum density.

= Primordial non-Gaussianity
{parent=Field theory in cosmology}
{wiki=Non-Gaussianity}

Primordial non-Gaussianity is any departure of the primordial perturbations from Gaussian random fields. Cubic interactions generate a nonzero three-point correlation function at leading order.

= Primordial bispectrum
{title2=$B(k_1,k_2,k_3)$}
{parent=Primordial non-Gaussianity}
{wiki=Bispectrum}

Statistical homogeneity defines the primordial bispectrum by
$$
\langle\phi_{\mathbf k_1}\phi_{\mathbf k_2}\phi_{\mathbf k_3}\rangle
=(2\pi)^3\delta^{(3)}(\mathbf k_1+\mathbf k_2+\mathbf k_3)
B(k_1,k_2,k_3).
$$

= Bispectrum from a time-derivative cubic scalar interaction
{parent=Primordial bispectrum}

For a massless scalar in <de Sitter spacetime> with interaction $S_{\rm int}=(\lambda/3!)\int dt\,d^3x\,a^3\dot\phi^2\phi$, the tree-level late-time <primordial bispectrum> is
$$
B=\frac{\lambda H^4}{12k_1^3k_2^3k_3^3K^2}
\sum_{\rm cyclic}k_j^2k_l^2(K+k_i),
\qquad K=k_1+k_2+k_3.
$$
It scales as $B(\mu k_1,\mu k_2,\mu k_3)=\mu^{-6}B(k_1,k_2,k_3)$.

= Shift-symmetry soft theorem for a scalar bispectrum
{parent=Primordial bispectrum}

If a scalar vacuum is invariant under an exact <scalar-field shift symmetry>, the Ward identity makes the leading soft bispectrum vanish:
$$
\lim_{q\to0}\frac{B_\phi(q,k,|\mathbf k+\mathbf q|)}{P_\phi(q)}=0.
$$

= Single-field inflation squeezed-limit consistency relation
{parent=Primordial bispectrum}

For a single-clock adiabatic inflationary background, a long <comoving curvature perturbation> acts on short modes as a spatial dilation. The squeezed bispectrum therefore obeys
$$
\lim_{q\to0}B_{\mathcal R}(q,k,|\mathbf k+\mathbf q|)
=-(n_s-1)P_{\mathcal R}(q)P_{\mathcal R}(k)
$$
at leading order in $q/k$.

= Primordial trispectrum
{title2=$T(\mathbf k_1,\mathbf k_2,\mathbf k_3,\mathbf k_4)$}
{parent=Primordial non-Gaussianity}
{wiki=Trispectrum}

The primordial trispectrum is the connected four-point function with its momentum-conserving delta function removed. Exchange diagrams split naturally into $s$, $t$, and $u$ channels according to which pair of external momenta meets at one cubic vertex.

= Parity-odd primordial trispectrum
{parent=Primordial trispectrum}

A parity-odd primordial trispectrum changes sign when all external momenta are reversed. Rotational invariance allows such a four-point structure because four momentum-conserving vectors can form a nonzero scalar triple product.

= Reality of a momentum-space scalar correlator
{parent=Primordial trispectrum}

For a real scalar field, $\phi(\mathbf k)^*=\phi(-\mathbf k)$. Its momentum-space $n$-point function therefore obeys
$$
\langle\phi(\mathbf k_1)\cdots\phi(\mathbf k_n)\rangle^*
=\langle\phi(-\mathbf k_1)\cdots\phi(-\mathbf k_n)\rangle.
$$
A parity-odd scalar correlator is consequently purely imaginary after removing its momentum-conserving delta function.

= Large-scale structure of the universe
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

The large-scale structure of the universe is the cosmic distribution of matter on scales where galaxies and halos form a web of clusters, filaments, sheets, and voids.

= Cosmological structure formation
{parent=Large-scale structure of the universe}
{wiki}

Cosmological structure formation is the growth of primordial density perturbations into sheets, filaments, halos, galaxies, and clusters under gravity.

= Zeldovich approximation
{c}
{parent=Cosmological structure formation}
{wiki}

The Zeldovich approximation maps a fluid element from its <Lagrangian coordinate> $\mathbf q$ to the comoving position $\mathbf x=\mathbf q+D(t)\mathbf s(\mathbf q)$, where $D$ is the <linear growth factor>. It follows the initial gravitational displacement along a straight comoving trajectory until trajectories cross.

= Cosmological Lagrangian displacement
{title2=$\boldsymbol\psi(\mathbf q,t)$}
{parent=Zeldovich approximation}

The cosmological Lagrangian displacement sends an initially labelled matter element from its <Lagrangian coordinate> $\mathbf q$ to $\mathbf x(\mathbf q,t)=\mathbf q+\boldsymbol\psi(\mathbf q,t)$. Its <derivative>[time derivative] determines the <peculiar velocity> through $\mathbf v=a\dot{\boldsymbol\psi}$.

= Deformation tensor in the Zel'dovich approximation
{title2=$d_{ij}$}
{parent=Zeldovich approximation}

For an irrotational displacement $\mathbf s=-\nabla_q\Phi$, the deformation tensor is the symmetric Hessian $d_{ij}=-\partial_i\partial_j\Phi$. Its real eigenvalues determine the principal collapse directions.

= Shell crossing
{parent=Zeldovich approximation}
{wiki}

Shell crossing occurs when the Lagrangian-to-Eulerian map ceases to be one-to-one. Its Jacobian determinant vanishes, the pressureless single-stream density formally diverges, and several streams subsequently occupy the same position.

= Cosmological caustic
{parent=Shell crossing}

A cosmological caustic is the image of a singular point of the Lagrangian map. The ideal collisionless density diverges there, although velocity dispersion and other small-scale physics regularize the singularity.

= Tidal torque theory
{parent=Cosmological structure formation}
{wiki}

Tidal torque theory explains the early <angular momentum> of a protohalo through the misalignment of its <mass second-moment tensor> $I_{ij}$ and the <Hessian matrix> $T_{ij}=\partial_i\partial_j\Phi$ of the surrounding <peculiar gravitational potential>. At leading order,
$$
J_i\propto a^2\dot D\,\epsilon_{ijk}T_{jl}I_{lk}.
$$

= Cosmic web
{parent=Cosmological structure formation}
{wiki}

The cosmic web is the network of matter sheets, filaments, nodes, and intervening voids produced by anisotropic gravitational collapse.

= Collisionless dark-matter Vlasov equation
{c}
{parent=Cosmological structure formation}
{wiki=Vlasov_equation}

For comoving position $\mathbf x$, canonical momentum $\mathbf p=am\mathbf x'$, and Newtonian potential $\phi$, the dark-matter phase-space density obeys
$$
\partial_\eta f+\frac{\mathbf p}{am}\mathbin\cdot\nabla_xf-am\nabla\phi\mathbin\cdot\nabla_pf=0.
$$

= Velocity-dispersion tensor of collisionless matter
{title2=$\sigma^{ij}$}
{parent=Collisionless dark-matter Vlasov equation}

The velocity-dispersion tensor is the second central moment of a phase-space distribution,
$$
\sigma^{ij}=\langle u^iu^j\rangle-v^iv^j.
$$
It contributes the stress-divergence term $\rho^{-1}\partial_j(\rho\sigma^{ij})$ to the Euler equation and vanishes in the ideal single-stream limit.

= Einstein-de Sitter universe
{c}
{parent=Large-scale structure of the universe}
{wiki=Einstein–de_Sitter_universe}

An Einstein-de Sitter universe is spatially flat and contains only pressureless matter. In conformal time, $a\propto\tau^2$ and $\mathcal H=a'/a=2/\tau$.

= Spherical secondary infall
{parent=Einstein-de Sitter universe}

Spherical secondary infall follows concentric collisionless shells around a primordial overdensity. Each shell initially expands with the Hubble flow, turns around, collapses, and subsequently crosses other shells.

= Turnaround radius
{title2=$r_*$}
{parent=Spherical secondary infall}
{wiki}

The turnaround radius of a shell is its maximum physical radius before collapse. At a given cosmic time, the currently turning shell defines a natural similarity length.

= Self-similar secondary infall
{parent=Spherical secondary infall}

If the initial fractional excess mass is a power law in enclosed mass, spherical secondary infall admits a similarity reduction in which radii scale by the current turnaround radius and masses by the mass of the currently turning shell.

= Standard perturbation theory in cosmology
{title2=SPT}
{parent=Large-scale structure of the universe}

Standard perturbation theory expands the density contrast and velocity divergence of a pressureless irrotational fluid in powers of the linear density field.

= Gaussian initial conditions
{c}
{parent=Standard perturbation theory in cosmology}

Gaussian initial conditions make every connected primordial correlation function above second order vanish. <Wick theorem> then reduces perturbative late-time correlation functions to sums of products of the linear power spectrum.

= Alpha mode-coupling kernel
{title2=$\alpha(\mathbf k_1,\mathbf k_2)$}
{parent=Standard perturbation theory in cosmology}

The continuity-equation kernel is
$$
\alpha(\mathbf k_1,\mathbf k_2)
=\frac{(\mathbf k_1+\mathbf k_2)\mathbin\cdot\mathbf k_1}{k_1^2}.
$$
Its symmetrization is $\alpha_s=1+(\mathbf k_1\mathbin\cdot\mathbf k_2)(k_1^{-2}+k_2^{-2})/2$.

= Beta mode-coupling kernel
{title2=$\beta(\mathbf k_1,\mathbf k_2)$}
{parent=Standard perturbation theory in cosmology}

The Euler-equation kernel is the symmetric function
$$
\beta(\mathbf k_1,\mathbf k_2)
=\frac{|\mathbf k_1+\mathbf k_2|^2
(\mathbf k_1\mathbin\cdot\mathbf k_2)}{2k_1^2k_2^2}.
$$

= Standard perturbation theory density kernel
{title2=$F_n$}
{parent=Standard perturbation theory in cosmology}

The symmetrized kernel $F_n$ convolves $n$ linear density fields to produce the $n$th-order density perturbation. In an Einstein-de Sitter universe,
$$
F_2=\frac57\alpha_s+\frac27\beta.
$$

= Ultraviolet softness of the second-order density kernel
{parent=Standard perturbation theory density kernel}

At fixed external momentum $\mathbf k$, mass and momentum conservation imply
$$
F_2(\mathbf q,\mathbf k-\mathbf q)=O(k^2/q^2)
$$
as $q\to\infty$. In particular, the constant term in the hard-momentum expansion must cancel.

= Unsmoothed matter-density reduced skewness
{title2=$S_3=34/7$}
{parent=Standard perturbation theory density kernel}

For <Gaussian initial conditions> and the Einstein-de Sitter second-order <standard perturbation theory density kernel>, the unsmoothed <density contrast> obeys
$$
\langle\delta^3\rangle=\frac{34}{7}\sigma^4+O(\sigma^6),
\qquad \sigma^2=\langle\delta^2\rangle.
$$
There are three choices of the second-order field and two connected <Wick contractions>; the angular average of $F_2$ is $5/7+(2/7)/3=17/21$. Thus the reduced <skewness> is $6(17/21)=34/7$. Smoothing changes this result, and the unsmoothed calculation requires a finite <variance>.

= Tree-level connected matter five-point function
{parent=Standard perturbation theory density kernel}

For Gaussian initial conditions, the tree-level connected matter five-point function has perturbative-order partitions
$$
4+1+1+1+1,
\qquad 3+2+1+1+1,
\qquad 2+2+2+1+1.
$$
They correspond respectively to a four-valent star, an $F_3$--$F_2$ tree, and a chain of three $F_2$ vertices. Every topology contains four linear power spectra.

= One-loop matter power spectrum
{parent=Standard perturbation theory in cosmology}

For Gaussian linear perturbations, the one-loop matter power spectrum is
$$
P_{\rm 1-loop}=a^2P+a^4(P_{22}+2P_{13}),
$$
where $P_{22}$ correlates two second-order fields and $P_{13}$ correlates a linear field with a third-order field.

= P22 contribution to the one-loop matter power spectrum
{title2=$P_{22}(k)$}
{c}
{parent=One-loop matter power spectrum}

The $P_{22}$ contribution correlates two second-order density fields. For symmetrized kernels,
$$
P_{22}(k)=2\int\frac{d^3q}{(2\pi)^3}F_2(\mathbf q,\mathbf k-\mathbf q)^2P(q)P(|\mathbf k-\mathbf q|).
$$

= P13 contribution to the one-loop matter power spectrum
{title2=$P_{13}(k)$}
{c}
{parent=One-loop matter power spectrum}

The $P_{13}$ contribution correlates a linear density field with a third-order field:
$$
P_{13}(k)=3P(k)\int\frac{d^3q}{(2\pi)^3}F_3(\mathbf k,\mathbf q,-\mathbf q)P(q).
$$
Both orderings contribute $2P_{13}$ to the power spectrum.

= Infrared cancellation in large-scale structure
{parent=One-loop matter power spectrum}

At equal time, leading loop contributions from a very long-wavelength displacement cancel between perturbative diagrams. This is required by the <equivalence principle>, because a coherent long displacement merely translates short-scale structure.

= Scale-free matter power spectrum
{parent=One-loop matter power spectrum}

A scale-free linear matter spectrum has $P(k)=Ak^n$. Dimensional analysis then makes its one-loop $P_{22}$ contribution proportional to $A^2k^{2n+3}$ whenever the loop integral converges.

= Standard perturbation theory velocity kernel
{title2=$G_n$}
{parent=Standard perturbation theory in cosmology}

The symmetrized kernel $G_n$ convolves $n$ linear density fields to produce the $n$th-order peculiar-velocity divergence in cosmological standard perturbation theory.

= One-loop matter bispectrum
{parent=Standard perturbation theory in cosmology}

For Gaussian initial conditions, the matter bispectrum through one loop is the sum of the tree diagram $B_{211}$ and the four one-loop topologies $B_{222}$, $B_{321}^{I}$, $B_{321}^{II}$, and $B_{411}$.

= One-loop matter trispectrum
{title2=$T_{\rm 1-loop}$}
{parent=Standard perturbation theory in cosmology}

For Gaussian initial conditions, the one-loop connected matter trispectrum contains the perturbative-order classes $T_{5111}$, $T_{4211}$, $T_{3311}$, $T_{3221}$, and $T_{2222}$. Each class includes every inequivalent external-leg assignment and Wick contraction.

= Effective field theory of large-scale structure
{title2=EFTofLSS}
{parent=Large-scale structure of the universe}
{wiki}

The effective field theory of large-scale structure represents unresolved short-scale dynamics by an effective stress tensor. Its leading deterministic correction is proportional to $k^2\delta^{(1)}$ and renormalizes ultraviolet-sensitive loop contributions.

= Effective sound-speed counterterm in large-scale structure
{title2=$-2c_{\rm eff}^2k^2P(k)$}
{parent=Effective field theory of large-scale structure}

The leading deterministic power-spectrum counterterm in the effective field theory of large-scale structure is proportional to $-2c_{\rm eff}^2k^2P(k)$. Its coefficient depends on the coarse-graining convention and runs so that it cancels the cutoff dependence of the one-loop $P_{13}$ contribution.

= Ultraviolet sensitivity of the one-loop matter power spectrum
{parent=Effective field theory of large-scale structure}

For internal momentum $q\gg k$, the <P22 contribution to the one-loop matter power spectrum> starts at $k^4$, while twice the <P13 contribution to the one-loop matter power spectrum> starts at $k^2P(k)$. The latter has the same momentum dependence as the <effective sound-speed counterterm in large-scale structure>. Short-scale stochastic contributions start at $k^4$ by mass and <momentum conservation>.

= Bias expansion
{parent=Effective field theory of large-scale structure}

A bias expansion expresses a tracer density, such as a galaxy or halo density, as a renormalized sum of local and nonlocal operators built from matter density, tidal fields, derivatives, and stochastic fields. Its coefficients encode unresolved tracer formation physics.

= Galaxy bias
{title2=$b$}
{parent=Bias expansion}
{wiki}

Galaxy bias describes how fluctuations in galaxy number density differ from fluctuations in the underlying matter density. On sufficiently large scales, the leading deterministic relation is $\delta_g=b_1\delta_m$.

= Lagrangian-to-Eulerian linear bias relation
{parent=Bias expansion}

Number conservation under the displacement from Lagrangian coordinates to Eulerian coordinates gives
$$
\boxed{b_1^{(E)}=1+b_1^{(L)}}.
$$
The added one is the linear matter-density response of the coordinate Jacobian.

= Local quadratic galaxy bias
{parent=Bias expansion}

The simplest nonlinear local bias model is
$$
\delta_g=b_1\delta+\frac{b_2}{2}(\delta^2-\langle\delta^2\rangle).
$$
For Gaussian matter fluctuations, its leading galaxy three-point function is proportional to $b_1^2b_2$.

= Galaxy bispectrum
{title2=$B_g(k_1,k_2,k_3)$}
{parent=Local quadratic galaxy bias}

The galaxy bispectrum is the connected momentum-space three-point function of the galaxy density contrast after removing its momentum-conserving delta function.

= Dark-matter halo
{parent=Large-scale structure of the universe}
{wiki=Dark_matter_halo}

A dark-matter halo is a gravitationally bound concentration of dark matter that hosts galaxies and larger structures.

= Virial radius of a dark-matter halo
{title2=$r_v$}
{parent=Dark-matter halo}

The virial radius of a dark-matter halo is conventionally the radius within which the mean density is a chosen overdensity $v$ times a reference critical or matter density.

= Virial mass of a dark-matter halo
{title2=$M_v$}
{parent=Virial radius of a dark-matter halo}

If the virial radius is defined relative to critical density $\rho_c$, the virial mass is
$$
M_v=\frac{4\pi}{3}v\rho_cr_v^3.
$$

= Concentration of a dark-matter halo
{title2=$c$}
{parent=Dark-matter halo}

= Halo concentration
{synonym}

The concentration of a spherical dark-matter halo compares its outer radius with an inner scale radius. For a <Navarro--Frenk--White profile>, $c=r_v/r_s$.

= Mass-concentration relation of dark-matter haloes
{parent=Concentration of a dark-matter halo}

The mass-concentration relation describes the statistical dependence of halo concentration on virial mass and epoch. In hierarchical cold-dark-matter cosmologies, lower-mass haloes generally formed earlier against a denser background and are therefore more concentrated at fixed epoch.

= Navarro--Frenk--White profile
{title2=NFW profile}
{c}
{parent=Dark-matter halo}
{wiki}

The Navarro--Frenk--White profile is
$$
\rho(r)=\frac{\rho_s}{(r/r_s)(1+r/r_s)^2}.
$$
It has an inner $r^{-1}$ density cusp and an outer $r^{-3}$ tail.

= Scale radius of a Navarro--Frenk--White profile
{title2=$r_s$}
{parent=Navarro--Frenk--White profile}

The NFW scale radius is where the logarithmic density slope equals $-2$.

= Characteristic density of a Navarro--Frenk--White profile
{title2=$\rho_s$}
{parent=Navarro--Frenk--White profile}

The NFW characteristic density fixes the normalization of the halo profile at a given scale radius.

= Cusp--core problem
{parent=Navarro--Frenk--White profile}
{wiki}

The cusp--core problem is the tension between the central cusps of collisionless cold-dark-matter halo profiles and the approximately constant-density cores inferred for many dwarf and low-surface-brightness galaxies.

= Dark-matter halo merger
{parent=Dark-matter halo}

A dark-matter halo merger joins previously distinct <dark-matter halos>. Orbital <angular momentum> can become internal halo spin, while escaping material carries away some mass and <angular momentum>. Halo mergers and accretion are important reasons that early <tidal torque theory> is only an approximate predictor of the spin of a later virialized remnant.

= Top-hat filter
{parent=Dark-matter halo}
{wiki}

A top-hat filter averages a field with uniform weight inside a ball and zero weight outside it.

= Spherical-collapse model
{parent=Dark-matter halo}

= Spherical collapse
{synonym}

The spherical-collapse model applies a spherical <top-hat filter> to an overdense region and predicts collapse when its linearly extrapolated smoothed overdensity exceeds the threshold $\delta_c\simeq1.686$ in an Einstein-de Sitter universe.

= Uniform-sphere collapse with a cosmological constant
{parent=Spherical-collapse model}

For fixed mass $M$, a uniform sphere of physical radius $R$ has $W_G=-3GM^2/(5R)$ and $W_\Lambda=-\Lambda MR^2/10$, using the convention in which its outward acceleration is $\Lambda r/3$. The <virial theorem> reads $2T+W_G=2W_\Lambda$. Defining $x=R_f/R_{\rm ta}$ and $\eta=\Lambda/(4\pi G\rho_{\rm ta})$, <conservation of energy> gives $2\eta x^3-(2+\eta)x+1=0$. The collapsing branch has $x=1/2-\eta/8+O(\eta^2)$. For small positive <cosmological constant>, it is more compact at fixed turnaround data, even though the outward force inhibits turnaround and collapse.

= Linear spherical-collapse threshold
{title2=$\delta_c$}
{parent=Spherical-collapse model}

In an Einstein-de Sitter universe, extrapolating the linear growing mode to the time when the spherical top-hat solution collapses gives
$$
\delta_c=\frac3{20}(12\pi)^{2/3}\simeq1.686.
$$

= Halo peak height
{title2=$\nu$}
{parent=Spherical-collapse model}

The peak height of a halo of mass $M$ is $\nu=\delta_c/\sigma(M)$, where $\sigma^2(M)$ is the variance of the linear density field smoothed on the corresponding mass scale.

= Press-Schechter formalism
{c}
{parent=Spherical-collapse model}
{wiki=Press–Schechter_formalism}

The Press-Schechter formalism estimates the halo abundance from the Gaussian probability that a smoothed linear overdensity crosses the spherical-collapse threshold, with a factor of two enforcing total mass conservation.

= Press-Schechter halo mass function
{c}
{parent=Press-Schechter formalism}

The Press-Schechter differential abundance is
$$
\frac{dn}{dM}
=-\sqrt{\frac2\pi}\frac{\bar\rho}{M^2}
\nu e^{-\nu^2/2}\frac{d\log\sigma}{d\log M}.
$$

= Peak-background split
{parent=Dark-matter halo}
{wiki}

The peak-background split decomposes density fluctuations into long- and short-wavelength parts. A long overdensity lowers the effective local collapse threshold and thereby changes the abundance of halos.

= Linear Lagrangian halo bias
{title2=$b_L(M)$}
{parent=Peak-background split}

Linear Lagrangian halo bias is the fractional response of halo abundance to a long-wavelength overdensity in the initial coordinates. For Press-Schechter halos, $b_L=(\nu^2-1)/\delta_c$.

= Linear Eulerian halo bias
{title2=$b(M)$}
{parent=Linear Lagrangian halo bias}

Mapping matter and halos from their initial positions adds the matter displacement contribution, giving $b=1+b_L$. Mass conservation requires the mass-weighted consistency relation $\int(dn/dM)(M/\bar\rho)b(M)dM=1$.

= Halo model
{parent=Dark-matter halo}
{wiki}

The halo model represents the matter density as a sum of normalized halo profiles and decomposes its power spectrum into same-halo and distinct-halo contributions.

= One-halo term
{title2=$P_{1h}$}
{parent=Halo model}

The one-halo term comes from pairs of mass elements in the same halo:
$$
P_{1h}(k)=\int dM\,\frac{dn}{dM}
\left(\frac{M}{\bar\rho}\right)^2|\widetilde u(k|M)|^2.
$$

= Two-halo term
{title2=$P_{2h}$}
{parent=Halo model}

At linear order in halo correlations,
$$
P_{2h}(k)=
\left[\int dM\,\frac{dn}{dM}\frac{M}{\bar\rho}
b(M)\widetilde u(k|M)\right]^2P_{\rm lin}(k).
$$

= Cosmic microwave background
{title2=CMB}
{c}
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Cosmic_microwave_background}

= Cosmic microwave background last-scattering surface
{title2=CMB last-scattering surface}
{c}
{parent=Cosmic microwave background}

The cosmic microwave background last-scattering surface is the shell from which the <photons> observed in the <cosmic microwave background> last scattered around <photon decoupling>.

= Cosmic microwave background anisotropy
{title2=CMB anisotropy}
{c}
{parent=Cosmic microwave background}

Cosmic microwave background anisotropies are direction-dependent perturbations of the relic radiation temperature and polarization.

= CMB lensing potential
{title2=$\psi$}
{c}
{parent=Cosmic microwave background anisotropy}

The CMB lensing potential is the line-of-sight projection of gravitational potential that remaps observed CMB directions by the deflection angle $\nabla\psi$.

= Cosmic microwave background power spectrum
{title2=CMB power spectrum}
{c}
{parent=Cosmic microwave background anisotropy}

= CMB angular power spectrum
{c}
{synonym}

The CMB angular power spectrum decomposes the variance of temperature or polarization anisotropy by angular multipole. Its large-angle plateau and acoustic peaks encode primordial perturbations and photon-baryon evolution.

= Cosmic microwave background polarization
{title2=CMB polarization}
{c}
{parent=Cosmic microwave background anisotropy}
{wiki}

Thomson scattering of a local radiation quadrupole generates linear cosmic microwave background polarization. Its acoustic phase follows the photon velocity and is shifted by one quarter-period relative to the monopole temperature oscillation.

= Photon temperature multipole
{title2=$\Theta_\ell$}
{parent=Cosmic microwave background anisotropy}

For Fourier wavevector $\mathbf k$ and photon direction $\widehat{\mathbf p}$, the temperature perturbation is expanded as
$$
\Theta(\mu)=\sum_{\ell=0}^{\infty}(-i)^\ell(2\ell+1)
\Theta_\ell P_\ell(\mu),
\qquad
\mu=\widehat{\mathbf k}\mathbin\cdot\widehat{\mathbf p}.
$$

= Photon monopole
{title2=$\Theta_0$}
{parent=Photon temperature multipole}

The photon monopole is the direction-averaged fractional temperature perturbation. In Newtonian gauge its gravitationally observable effective temperature is the Sachs-Wolfe combination $\Theta_0+\Psi$.

= Sachs-Wolfe combination
{c}
{parent=Photon monopole}

In Newtonian gauge, $\Theta_0+\Psi$ combines the intrinsic photon temperature perturbation with the gravitational redshift at emission. With constant potentials and negligible baryon loading it oscillates as $\cos(kr_s)$ for adiabatic initial conditions.

= Sachs-Wolfe effect
{c}
{parent=Sachs-Wolfe combination}
{wiki}

The Sachs-Wolfe effect is the large-angle CMB temperature anisotropy produced by intrinsic photon-temperature perturbations and gravitational redshift at last scattering. For an adiabatic mode during matter domination, $\Theta_0+\Psi=\Psi/3$ on superhorizon scales.

= Doppler CMB anisotropy
{c}
{parent=Sachs-Wolfe combination}

The Doppler CMB anisotropy is the line-of-sight velocity contribution from the last-scattering plasma. With the convention used here it contributes $-\widehat{\mathbf n}\mathbin\cdot\mathbf v_e$ to the observed fractional temperature perturbation.

= Integrated Sachs-Wolfe effect
{c}
{parent=Sachs-Wolfe combination}
{wiki}

The integrated Sachs-Wolfe effect is the CMB temperature shift produced when the gravitational potentials traversed by a photon evolve with time. Its line-of-sight source is proportional to $\Phi'+\Psi'$.

= Photon dipole
{title2=$\Theta_1$}
{parent=Photon temperature multipole}

The photon dipole represents the bulk velocity of the radiation. In tight coupling to baryons, $v_b=-3\Theta_1$ under the convention used here.

= Photon quadrupole
{title2=$\Theta_2$}
{parent=Photon temperature multipole}

The photon quadrupole is the leading anisotropy that sources linear polarization through Thomson scattering.

= Photon-baryon fluid
{parent=Cosmic microwave background anisotropy}

Before recombination, frequent Thomson scattering couples photons, electrons, and baryons into an acoustic fluid.

= Thomson scattering
{c}
{parent=Photon-baryon fluid}
{wiki}

Thomson scattering is the low-energy elastic scattering of electromagnetic radiation by a free charged particle. Before cosmological recombination, repeated photon-electron scattering couples photons to the baryon velocity and damps higher photon multipoles.

= Tight-coupling approximation
{parent=Photon-baryon fluid}
{wiki}

= Tight coupling
{synonym}

The tight-coupling approximation expands the photon-baryon Boltzmann equations in powers of $k/\Gamma$ and $\mathcal H/\Gamma$, where $\Gamma$ is the Thomson scattering rate.

= Photon-baryon sound speed
{title2=$c_s^2$}
{parent=Tight-coupling approximation}

For baryon-loading ratio $R=3\rho_b/(4\rho_\gamma)$, the photon-baryon sound speed is $c_s^2=1/[3(1+R)]$.

= Photon-baryon velocity slip
{title2=$s=v_\gamma-v_b$}
{parent=Tight-coupling approximation}

The velocity slip is the difference between the bulk velocities of photons and baryons. <Thomson scattering> suppresses it at a rate $\Gamma(1+R^{-1})$, where $R=3\rho_b/(4\rho_\gamma)$ and $\Gamma=-\dot\tau>0$. Its first correction in the <tight-coupling approximation> produces heat-conduction damping of acoustic waves.

= Photon quadrupole in tight coupling with polarization
{parent=Tight-coupling approximation}

For the expansion $\Theta(\mu)=\sum_\ell(-i)^\ell\Theta_\ell P_\ell(\mu)$ without a $(2\ell+1)$ factor, the leading polarization relation is $E_2=-\sqrt6\,\Theta_2/4$. The <photon Boltzmann hierarchy> then gives $\Theta_2=-8k\Theta_1/(9\dot\tau)$. This small <photon quadrupole> supplies the shear-viscosity part of <Silk damping>.

= Photon-baryon diffusion damping equation
{parent=Tight-coupling approximation}

When gravity and expansion can be neglected and $R$ is constant, the <photon monopole> satisfies
$$
\ddot\Theta_0+
\frac{k^2}{3(1+R)\Gamma}
\left(\frac{R^2}{1+R}+\frac{16}{15}\right)\dot\Theta_0+
\frac{k^2}{3(1+R)}\Theta_0=0.
$$
The first damping contribution comes from <photon-baryon velocity slip>; the second comes from the <photon quadrupole>. This is a local form of <Silk damping>.

= Sound horizon
{title2=$r_s$}
{parent=Photon-baryon fluid}
{wiki}

The comoving sound horizon is the distance traveled by an acoustic wave,
$$
r_s(\eta)=\int_0^\eta c_s(\eta')\,d\eta'.
$$

= Cosmic microwave background diffusion damping
{title2=CMB diffusion damping}
{c}
{parent=Photon-baryon fluid}

= Silk damping
{c}
{synonym}

Cosmic microwave background diffusion damping is the suppression of small-angular-scale anisotropy as photons random-walk out of overdense regions during the finite-width recombination epoch. It produces an approximately exponential damping tail in the <cosmic microwave background power spectrum> at multipoles $\ell\gtrsim1000$.

= Adiabatic initial conditions
{parent=Cosmic microwave background anisotropy}

= Adiabatic cosmological perturbation
{synonym}

Adiabatic initial conditions perturb every species by the same local time shift, so their relative number-density ratios are initially unperturbed. Photon acoustic modes then begin predominantly as displacement modes with cosine phase.

Equivalently, $\delta\rho_i/\dot{\bar\rho}_i$ is independent of the component $i$. The <non-adiabatic pressure perturbation> then vanishes, and the <comoving curvature perturbation> is conserved on <superhorizon scales> when anisotropic stress and gradient terms can be neglected.

= Cosmic microwave background acoustic peak
{title2=CMB acoustic peak}
{c}
{parent=Cosmic microwave background anisotropy}

Cosmic microwave background acoustic peaks arise from photon-baryon oscillations caught at successive extrema at recombination. Their approximate phase is set by $kr_s=n\pi$ and their angular positions measure the sound horizon relative to the distance to last scattering.

= Free-streaming photon Boltzmann equation
{c}
{parent=Cosmic microwave background anisotropy}

In the absence of collisions, a photon phase-space density is constant along its geodesic. Linearizing this statement gives a first-order transport equation for the direction-dependent temperature perturbation.

= Photon Boltzmann hierarchy
{title2=$\Theta_\ell$ hierarchy}
{parent=Free-streaming photon Boltzmann equation}

Expanding the direction-dependent photon temperature in <Legendre polynomials> turns its transport equation into an infinite hierarchy. Free streaming couples each multipole $\Theta_\ell$ only to its neighbours $\Theta_{\ell-1}$ and $\Theta_{\ell+1}$.

= Photon Euler equation
{parent=Photon Boltzmann hierarchy}

The dipole member of the photon Boltzmann hierarchy is the photon Euler equation. In Newtonian gauge and without collisions,
$$
\Theta_1'=\frac{k}{3}(\Theta_0+\Psi-2\Theta_2).
$$

= Line-of-sight solution for free-streaming photons
{parent=Free-streaming photon Boltzmann equation}

The line-of-sight solution integrates the gravitational source along the unperturbed photon trajectory. In Fourier space the integrating factor $e^{ik\mu\eta}$ accounts for free streaming between emission and observation.

= Photon Boltzmann equation with Thomson scattering
{c}
{parent=Free-streaming photon Boltzmann equation}

At linear order in Newtonian gauge, free streaming, gravitational redshift, and Thomson scattering combine into a first-order transport equation for the photon temperature perturbation. The collision term drives the radiation toward its monopole plus the electron bulk-velocity dipole.

= Cosmological optical depth
{title2=$\tau(\eta)$}
{parent=Photon Boltzmann equation with Thomson scattering}

The optical depth from conformal time $\eta$ to observation at $\eta_0$ is
$$
\tau(\eta)=\int_\eta^{\eta_0}\Gamma(\eta')\,d\eta'.
$$
The factor $e^{-\tau(\eta)}$ is the probability that a photon travels from $\eta$ to the observer without another scattering.

= Cosmological visibility function
{title2=$g(\eta)$}
{parent=Cosmological optical depth}
{wiki=Recombination_(cosmology)#Visibility_function}

The visibility function
$$
g(\eta)=\partial_\eta e^{-\tau(\eta)}
=\Gamma(\eta)e^{-\tau(\eta)}
$$
is the probability density for the conformal time of a CMB photon's last scattering.

= Cosmic microwave background line-of-sight solution
{title2=CMB line-of-sight solution}
{c}
{parent=Cosmological visibility function}

The CMB line-of-sight solution separates sharply localized last-scattering sources, weighted by the <cosmological visibility function>, from integrated gravitational sources weighted by $e^{-\tau}$ along the photon trajectory.

= Cosmological distance measure
{parent=Cosmology}

A cosmological distance measure converts an observed redshift, angle, flux, or travel time into a distance in an expanding spacetime. Different measures answer different operational questions and therefore need not agree numerically.

= Angular diameter distance
{title2=$d_A$}
{parent=Cosmological distance measure}
{wiki}

The angular diameter distance is defined by $d_A=x/\theta$ for a small transverse physical size $x$ subtending angle $\theta$. In a spatially flat <Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric>,
$$
d_A(z)=\frac{\chi(z)}{1+z},
$$
where $\chi$ is the <comoving radial distance>.

= Angular diameter distance turnover
{parent=Angular diameter distance}

In a standard expanding cosmology, $d_A(z)$ initially increases from zero but eventually decreases because the factor $(1+z)^{-1}$ dominates the bounded comoving distance. Beyond its maximum, a fixed physical object subtends a larger angle at a larger redshift.

= Cosmological standard ruler
{parent=Cosmological distance measure}

A cosmological standard ruler is a feature with a fixed known or redshift-independent physical or comoving size. Comparing its observed angular and radial extents constrains the expansion history even when its absolute size is treated as a nuisance parameter.

= Baryon acoustic oscillation
{title2=BAO}
{parent=Cosmological standard ruler}
{wiki}

Baryon acoustic oscillations are the relic spatial scale of sound waves in the pre-recombination <photon-baryon fluid>. The scale appears both as a preferred separation in galaxy clustering and as oscillatory structure in the <cosmological density power spectrum>.

= Transverse baryon acoustic oscillation measurement
{title2=Transverse BAO measurement}
{parent=Baryon acoustic oscillation}

For a comoving BAO scale $\chi_d$ perpendicular to the line of sight in a spatially flat universe,
$$
\Delta\theta=\frac{\chi_d}{\chi(z)}.
$$
Its redshift dependence constrains the shape of the distance--redshift relation.

= Radial baryon acoustic oscillation measurement
{title2=Radial BAO measurement}
{parent=Baryon acoustic oscillation}

For a small comoving BAO separation parallel to the line of sight,
$$
\Delta z=H(z)\chi_d
$$
in units with $c=1$. Combining this with a transverse measurement eliminates the unknown ruler size.

= Baryon acoustic oscillation in the matter power spectrum
{title2=BAO in the matter power spectrum}
{parent=Baryon acoustic oscillation}

The matter power spectrum contains low-amplitude oscillatory features because baryons preserve their pre-recombination acoustic phase and later transfer part of it gravitationally to the combined matter distribution.

= Alcock-Paczyński test
{c}
{parent=Cosmological standard ruler}
{wiki}

The Alcock-Paczyński test compares radial and transverse distance conversion using a feature that is statistically isotropic in comoving space.

= Alcock-Paczyński parameter
{title2=$F_{\rm AP}(z)=H(z)\chi(z)$}
{c}
{parent=Alcock-Paczyński test}

The Alcock-Paczyński parameter compares radial and transverse distance conversion at one redshift. An intrinsically isotropic comoving feature obeys
$$
\frac{\Delta z}{\Delta\theta}=H(z)\chi(z),
$$
so its unknown absolute size cancels.

= Lambda-CDM model
{title2=$\Lambda$CDM model}
{c}
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

The Lambda-CDM model combines a <cosmological constant> with <cold dark matter> and the standard baryonic and radiation components in an expanding <Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric>.

= Dark matter
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

Dark matter is matter inferred from its gravitational effects that does not emit, absorb, or scatter enough electromagnetic radiation to account for those effects as ordinary baryonic matter.

= Cold dark matter
{title2=CDM}
{parent=Dark matter}
{wiki}

Cold dark matter is nonrelativistic early enough that its primordial free-streaming length is small compared with the cosmological structures of interest.

= Warm dark matter
{title2=WDM}
{parent=Dark matter}
{wiki}

Warm dark matter has a primordial <collisionless free-streaming> length large enough to suppress the formation of low-mass haloes, while remaining nonrelativistic early enough to cluster on galactic and larger scales.

= Matter-radiation equality
{parent=Cosmology}

Matter-radiation equality is the epoch at which the background matter and radiation densities are equal. Its horizon wavenumber $k_{\rm eq}$ sets the turnover scale of the cold-dark-matter transfer function.

= Matter-radiation equality scale
{title2=$k_{\rm eq}$}
{parent=Matter-radiation equality}

The matter-radiation equality scale is the comoving horizon wavenumber at equality. Modes at larger wavenumber entered during radiation domination and had their matter growth suppressed.
